o entities are chasing the cat. .\n\nBut again, nothing about the cat\'s own chasing behavior unless there\'s a rule that applies to it. .\n\nWait, is there any rule that says if an entity is being chased by another, then it must chase itself? Not that I can see. .\n\nTherefore, the problem is whether the cat chases itself. Since there\'s no statement that says the cat does so, and no rule that would necessitate it or forbid it, the answer should be unknown (C). .\n\nWait, but wait another angle: The question is whether the statement is true, false, or unknown. "Unknown" would mean there\'s insufficient information. .\n\nBut maybe there\'s a way to derive it. Let me think again. .\n\nSuppose we assume that the cat chases itself. Then according to statement 14, the cat (which is chasing the cat) cannot chase the mouse. So if the cat chases itself, it can\'t chase the mouse. But is there any rule that would require the cat to chase the mouse? Let\'s see. .\n\nLooking at the statements, the cat eats the cow (statement 3). The mouse eats the bald eagle (statement 10). The cow eats the cat (statement 4). The bald eagle chases the cat (statement 1). There\'s no mention of the cat chasing the mouse. So the cat could or could not chase the mouse. .\n\nTherefore, if the cat were to chase itself, it would have to not chase the mouse. Since there\'s no rule forcing the cat to chase the mouse, that\'s acceptable. .\n\nAlternatively, if the cat doesn\'t chase itself, then statement 14 doesn\'t apply to it in that scenario. .\n\nSo since there\'s no information either way, the answer would be unknown. .\n\nBut wait, let\'s check the options again. The options are A) True, B) False, C) Unknown. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a contradiction if the cat were to chase itself. Let me think again. .\n\nSuppose the cat does chase itself. Then, according to statement 14, the cat (which is the chaser) cannot chase the mouse. So the cat cannot chase the mouse. But there\'s no rule that