again.\n\nLooking at statement 14: If something needs the cow and it chases the squirrel, then the cow is rough. .\n\nIf the lion (which needs the cow) chases the squirrel, then the cow is rough. But we don\'t know if the lion chases the squirrel. However, statement 15 says if something chases the squirrel and it is young, then the squirrel needs the cat. The squirrel is young, so if the lion chases the squirrel (since the lion is an entity that might do that), then the squirrel would need the cat. But the squirrel\'s need for the cat isn\'t mentioned in the original context except through this implication. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the squirrel needs the cat because of statement 15. Let me see: if something (say, the lion) chases the squirrel and the squirrel is young, then squirrel needs cat. .\n\nBut unless we have the lion chasing the squirrel, this doesn\'t trigger. .\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the squirrel chases the cow? That would be from statement 17, but that requires the squirrel visiting the cat and the cat being red. Since we don\'t know the cat\'s color, maybe that\'s not applicable.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the cow\'s visits can lead us somewhere. The cow is big, so it must visit the squirrel (statement 19). So the cow visits the squirrel. .\n\nWait, the cow\'s visits: the cow is big, so by 19, it visits the squirrel. So cow visits squirrel. .\n\nDoes that mean anything? .\n\nIf the cow visits the squirrel, then according to statement 16, if something visits the cow, then it is rough. Wait, no, statement 16 is about if something visits the cow, then that something is rough. Since the cow visits the squirrel, that doesn\'t involve visiting the cow. Wait, the cow\'s own visits are to the squirrel. .\n\nBut if someone else visits the cow, then that someone is rough. .\n\nNow, does any entity visit the cow? Let\'s see:.\n\nThe lion needs the cow, but does it visit the cow? Not stated. The squirrel visits the squirrel (if that\'s true), but not the cow. The cat visits the lion and the squirrel. The cow visits the squirrel. .\n\nSo unless the lion visits the cow, no one else does. .\n\nIf the lion visits the cow, then by statement 16, the lion is rough. But is there any reason to think the lion visits the cow?.\n\nThe lion needs the cow (statement 9). But needing doesn\'t imply visiting. However, perhaps through some other rule. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the cow\'s visit to the squirrel has implications. Since the cow visits the squirrel, and the squirrel is nice (so big), then perhaps some other rule applies. .\n\nAlternatively, let\'s see if we can find any contradictions or confirmations about the cow needing the squirrel. .\n\nThe question is whether the cow does NOT need the squirrel. So the statement is "The cow does not need the squirrel." We need to see if this is necessarily true, false, or unknown. .\n\nThe given info says the cow needs the cat. There\'s no direct mention of needing the squirrel, so unless there\'s a rule that forces the cow to need the squirrel, the answer might be "unknown". But maybe there\'s an indirect way.\n\nLooking at statement 14 again: If something (say, the lion) needs the cow and chases the squirrel, then the cow is rough. .\n\nSuppose we assume that the cow does need the squirrel. Would that lead to a contradiction?.\n\nAlternatively, suppose the cow does need the squirrel. Then, does any rule get triggered? .\n\nWait, the cow\'s needs are only stated as needing the cat. If it also needed the squirrel, that would be an additional need not mentioned, but maybe it\'s possible. .\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the squirrel\'s needs could imply something about the cow. The squirrel is nice (so big), so it must visit the squirrel (statement 19). Also, from statement 15, if anything chases the squirrel, then the squirrel needs the cat. .\n\nBut without knowing if anything chases the squirrel, that\'s not helpful yet. .\n\nAlternatively, let\'s see if the cow\'s visit to the squirrel (because