 mouse (Premise 14).  \n   - If something likes the mouse, it sees the mouse (Premise 16).  \n   - Other rules do not involve \"needs\" in a way that could create a reflexive dependency (e.g., needing itself).  \n\n### Analysis of \"The rabbit needs the rabbit\":  \n- **No Direct Statement**: The premises explicitly state the rabbit\u2019s needs (only the tiger), but there is no mention of the rabbit needing itself.  \n- **No Indirect Inference**: None of the conditional rules (e.g., Premises 13\u201321) lead to an inference that the rabbit needs itself. For example:  \n  - The rabbit\u2019s need for the tiger (Premise 10) does not trigger any rule that would create a need for itself.  \n  - Chains of dependencies (e.g., rabbit \u2192 tiger \u2192 mouse \u2192 rabbit) do not form a loop that justifies a self-need.  \n- **Default Assumption**: Since no rule or premise supports the claim, the statement cannot be true.  \n\n### Conclusion:  \nThe statement \"The rabbit needs the rabbit\" is **false** because it is neither stated nor derivable from the premises.  \n\n**Answer:** B) False",
