ntrapositive would be "If Bob is not furry, then Bob is not white." But since we know Bob is furry (from being smart), the contrapositive doesn\'t help us here. So the contrapositive tells us that if he\'s not white, then.. but since we know he is furry, the contrapositive\'s antecedent (not furry) is false, so the contrapositive doesn\'t give us info about his whiteness.\n\nTherefore, the only thing we can say about Bob\'s whiteness is that there\'s no information provided in the premises that directly states he is or isn\'t white. So is there any indirect way?.\n\nWait, let me think again. Let\'s see if any of the other premises might require Bob to have a property that would conflict with being white. For instance, is there a rule that if someone is white, they must not be something else?.\n\nLooking through the premises again:.\n\nPremise 19: If Fiona is red and white, then Fiona is smart. But Fiona is already smart, so that doesn\'t impose a restriction on Fiona\'s whiteness except that if she were both red and white, it\'s okay as long as she\'s smart (which she is). But this doesn\'t affect Bob.\n\nHmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the fact that all smart things are furry (premise 11), and since Bob is smart, he is furry. Since he\'s furry, premise 12 says all furry things are red, so Bob is red (which matches premise 2). So that\'s consistent. But no connection to white.\n\nAlternatively, could there be an assumption that being white and red are mutually exclusive? The problem doesn\'t state that. Unless there\'s a rule like "All red things are not white" or something, but I don\'t see any such rule. So Bob could be both red and white unless prohibited.\n\nTherefore, since there\'s no information that says Bob cannot be white, and no rule that would contradict him being white, then the statement "Bob is not white" would be unknown. Because we can\'t confirm he is white, nor can we confirm he isn\'t. Wait, but maybe there\'s a way to prove he isn\'t white?.\n\nWait, let me think again. Suppose Bob is white. Then, according to premise 14, he must be furry, which he is. So that\'s okay. But is there another consequence?.\n\nAlternatively, if Bob is white, does that affect any other premises?.\n\nLet me think about the other rules. For example, premise 15: All red, rough things are big. If Bob is rough, then since he is red, he must be big. But if he is rough, that would require him to be round (from premise 13). But again, we don\'t know if he\'s round.\n\nAlternatively, suppose Bob is white and red, but not rough. Then that\'s okay, because premise 15 only applies to red AND rough things. So unless he is rough, being red and white doesn\'t require him to be big.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s another way to determine if Bob is rough. Since Bob is smart (3), and premise 16 says all rough things are smart. Wait, premise 16 is "All rough things are smart", which means that if something is rough, it must be smart. But the converse isn\'t necessarily true. So just because Bob is smart doesn\'t mean he\'s rough. So Bob could be smart without being rough. So no, that doesn\'t help.\n\nHmm, so after going through all the premises, I don\'t see any rule that would force Bob to not be white, nor any rule that would require him to be white. The only thing we know is that if he were white, he would have to be furry, which he is. But there\'s no other constraints.\n\nWait, but maybe looking at other properties. Let\'s see, is there a way Bob could be forced to be big?.\n\nPremise 15: All red, rough things are big. If Bob were rough and red, then he must be big. But unless he\'s rough, that doesn\'t apply. But if Bob is round (not stated), then he would be rough (premise 13). But since Bob\'s roundness isn\'t given, we can\'t assume that.\n\nAlternatively, if Bob is big, does that affect anything? Let me see premise 18: If Bob is round AND big, then he\'s furry. Since he\'s already furry, that\'s okay. But again, unless we know his roundness or bigness, this doesn\'t help.\n\nWait, Fiona is big (8). Erin is rough (6). But none of these relate directly to Bob\'s whiteness.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider if there\'s any contradiction if we suppose Bob is white. Suppose Bob is white. Then:.\n\n- Bob is white, red, smart, furry (from being smart), and that\'s it unless other properties are inferred.\n\nBut there\'s no rule that would create a problem here. So that\'s acceptable.\n\nAlternatively, if Bob is not white, then the statement "Bob is not white" is true. But we can\'t confirm that either, because there\'s no rule that says he must not be white.\n\nTherefore, since there\'s no information that directly states Bob\'s whiteness or gives a contradiction if he were white, the correct answer should be "Unknown", which is option C.\n\nWait, but wait a second. Let me check again for any possible oversight. Let me see if there\'s any other premise that might have an effect.\n\nLooking at premise 17: If Fiona is furry then Fiona is red. Since Fiona is furry (9), then Fiona must be red. So Fiona is red, which is inferred, but not stated. So Fiona is red. .\n\nPremise 19: If Fiona is red and white, then Fiona is smart. Since Fiona is already smart (10), even if she were white, that\'s okay. But again, Fiona\'s whiteness isn\'t mentioned, so that doesn\'t affect Bob.\n\nAnother angle: Let\'s check if being white is a property that\'s mentioned elsewhere. The only premises that mention white are 14 (Bob\'s case) and 19 (Fiona\'s case). There\'s no general rule about white things, only those two conditionals. .\n\nTherefore, since there\'s no information that Bob is white or not, and no contradictions if he is or isn\'t, the statement "Bob is not white" can\'t be confirmed as true or false. Therefore, the answer should be C) Unknown.\n\nWait, but wait another thought: The question is asking if "Bob is not white" is true, false, or unknown. If we can\'t prove he is white, then "Bob is not white" could be true. But the problem might require that for it to be true, we must have proof that he is not white, otherwise it\'s unknown. .\n\nIn logic, unless there\'s a rule that explicitly states he isn\'t white, or that being white would lead to a contradiction, we can\'t conclude it\'s true. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 14%|#4        | 84/600 [1:06:49<4:46:33, 33.32s/it]
