ause it eats the squirrel, premise17), so the cat must be cold. .\n\nNow, premise18: If someone is cold then they eat the cow. .\n\nThe cat is cold (from above), so the cat must eat the cow. .\n\nTherefore, the cat eats the cow. .\n\nNow, does eating the cow have any implications? .\n\nPremise16: If someone eats the cow then they see the rabbit. .\n\nSo the cat eats the cow (from premise18 implication), so the cat must see the rabbit. .\n\nAh! Here we go. .\n\nSo the cat now must see the rabbit. .\n\nBecause the cat is cold (from being rough because it eats the squirrel), so by premise18, it eats the cow. Then by premise16, eating the cow makes the cat see the rabbit. .\n\nTherefore, the cat sees the rabbit. .\n\nNow, applying premise14: If someone sees the rabbit, then they are not round. .\n\nThe cat now sees the rabbit (from the above chain), so the cat must not be round. .\n\nTherefore, the cat is not round. .\n\nSo the statement "The cat is not round" is true. .\n\nWait, let me retrace the steps to confirm:.\n\n1. Squirrel is cold (premise8). .\n\n2. By premise18: cold → eat the cow. So squirrel must eat the cow. .\n\n3. Squirrel eats the cow → by premise16, must see the rabbit. So squirrel sees rabbit. .\n\n4. Since squirrel sees rabbit, premise14 applies: squirrel must not be round. .\n\n5. Cat eats the squirrel (premise1) → by premise17, cat is rough. .\n\n6. Rough → cold (premise13). So cat is cold. .\n\n7. Cold cat → must eat the cow (premise18). .\n\n8. Eating the cow → must see the rabbit (premise16). So cat sees rabbit. .\n\n9. Cat sees rabbit → by premise14, cat must not be round. .\n\nTherefore, the cat is not round → the statement is true (option A). .\n\nWait, but let me check for any possible contradictions or missed steps. .\n\nIs there any possibility that the cat is green, which would affect premise15? .\n\nPremise15 says if someone sees the squirrel and is not green, then they need the squirrel. .\n\nThe cat sees the squirrel (premise2), so if 