ed. \n\nWait, let me check all conditionals one more time:\n\nStatement 14: If visits cat \u2192 likes cat. So lion likes cat. \n\nStatement 17: If likes cat \u2192 chases squirrel. So lion must chase squirrel. \n\nStatement 19: If chases lion \u2192 lion chases cow. But who chases the lion? Not mentioned. \n\nStatement 20: If chases squirrel \u2192 squirrel visits cat. \n\nBut no, the squirrel's visits aren't mentioned. \n\nStatement 16: If likes cow and visits cow \u2192 cow visits cat. \n\nCat likes cow (3) but doesn't visit cow, so no. \n\nStatement 15: If likes squirrel and big \u2192 squirrel likes lion. \n\nCat is big, but does it like the squirrel? Not stated. \n\nStatement 13: If visits squirrel \u2192 likes cat. \n\nBut no one visits the squirrel. \n\nStatement 12: Squirrel likes cow. \n\nStatement 10: Lion is rough. \n\nStatement 21: If cow is rough \u2192 cow chases cat. But cow's roughness not stated. \n\nSo after all this, I think the correct answer is C) Unknown. The user probably thought there was a rule against it, but I don't see it.\n</think>\n\nTo determine whether \"The cat likes the lion\" is true, false, or unknown, we analyze the given statements and their implications:\n\n1. **Direct Statements**:\n   - The cat likes the cow (statement 3).\n   - The lion likes the cat because the lion visits the cat (statement 11), and visiting the cat requires liking the cat (statement 14). However, this only establishes the lion\u2019s feelings toward the cat, not the cat\u2019s feelings toward the lion.\n\n2. **Conditional Implications**:\n   - **Statement 17**: If something likes the cat, it must chase the squirrel. The lion likes the cat (from above), so it must chase the squirrel. This does not affect the cat\u2019s feelings toward the lion.\n   - **Other conditionals** (e.g., statements 13, 15, 16, 19, 20, and 21) involve relationships between other animals but do not directly or indirectly link the cat\u2019s feelings toward the lion.\n\n3. **No Dire