if we can determine that.\n\nCharlie is young. If Charlie were white, then by premise 12 (Young and White → Round), he would have to be round, hence big. But Charlie\'s color isn\'t stated. So unless we can determine his color, we can\'t say.\n\nWait, Charlie\'s color is not given, except that he\'s young. Since all blue things are young, but he could be another color. So unless there\'s a rule that requires him to be a certain color, we can\'t assume. So maybe Charlie is not blue, not white, etc. So his color is unknown.\n\nTherefore, the possibility remains that Charlie is big or not. But we need to see if there\'s any other path.\n\nAnother angle: Let\'s see if Charlie can be smart or not. Suppose Charlie is not smart. Then, since quiet → smart (premise 9), if Charlie were quiet, he would have to be smart. So ¬Smart → ¬Quiet (contrapositive). So if Charlie is not smart, he can\'t be quiet. But that doesn\'t directly help.\n\nWait, let\'s think of all possibilities for Charlie\'s properties:.\n\nWe know Charlie is young. Let\'s see possible scenarios:.\n\nScenario 1: Charlie is big.\n\nThen, by premise 14, he is not smart. .\n\nIs there any other constraints? If he is big, but not blue, then he doesn\'t have to be quiet. But if he is blue, then big and blue would require quiet, leading to smart, which contradicts premise 14. So if he\'s big, he must not be blue. But his color is unknown. So possible, but maybe there\'s another way.\n\nAlternatively, if Charlie is big and not blue, then okay. But does being big have any other implications?.\n\nScenario 2: Charlie is not big.\n\nThen the statement "Charlie is not big" is true. But can we confirm that?.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a way to force Charlie to be big. Let\'s see.\n\nIf Charlie were round, he must be big (premise 15). So if we can prove Charlie is round, then he must be big, making the statement false.\n\nIs there any reason to think Charlie is round?.\n\nCharlie is young. If Charlie were white, then