. So if Charlie is quiet, he must be smart. But if he\'s not quiet, then his smartness is unknown.\n\nThis is getting a bit tangled. Let me try to structure this.\n\nWe need to determine if Charlie is big or not. Let\'s see possible cases.\n\nCase 1: Charlie is big.\n\nThen, from premise 14, since he is young and big, he is not smart.\n\nNow, can Charlie be quiet?.\n\nIf Charlie is big and blue, then premise 11 requires him to be quiet. But if he is big and blue, then being quiet would require him to be smart (premise 9). But premise 14 says he\'s not smart. So that\'s a contradiction. Therefore, Charlie cannot be both big and blue. Hence, if Charlie is big, he must not be blue.\n\nBut Charlie\'s color isn\'t specified. So that\'s possible. So Charlie could be big and not blue, thus not violating any rules.\n\nAlternatively, if Charlie is big and not blue, then he doesn\'t have to be quiet (since premise 11 only applies to blue things). So he can be quiet or not. If he\'s not quiet, then premise 9 doesn\'t require him to be smart, so being not smart is okay. So this scenario is possible.\n\nTherefore, Charlie can be big, provided he\'s not blue. So that\'s possible.\n\nCase 2: Charlie is not big.\n\nThen the statement is true. But can we confirm that?.\n\nAre there any premises that require Charlie to be big? Let\'s see.\n\nIf Charlie were round, then he must be big (premise 15). So if he is round, he must be big. But we don\'t know if he\'s round.\n\nAlternatively, if Charlie is white, then by premise 12 (Young and White → Round), he must be round, hence big. But again, we don\'t know if he\'s white.\n\nSince Charlie\'s color is unknown, we can\'t assume he\'s white or round unless forced. Since there\'s no information about his color beyond being young (which could be from being blue, but he might not be), we can\'t conclude he\'s white or any other color.\n\nTherefore, Charlie could be not big (Case 2), or big (Case 1). There\'s no contradiction in either case, so it\'s possible for Charlie to be either. Hence, the statement "Charlie is not big" could be true or false, so the answer would be unknown (option C).\n\nWait, but let me check again. Is there any other rule that might constrain this?.\n\nAnother angle: Let\'s see if there\'s a way to force Charlie to be not big.\n\nSuppose Charlie is not big. Then he can be smart or not. Since there\'s no rule requiring him to be smart, that\'s okay.\n\nAlternatively, if Charlie is smart, then he could be quiet (since quiet → smart, but not the other way). But quiet would require him to be big and blue (from premise 11), which he isn\'t. So if he\'s smart, he might not be quiet. So that\'s okay.\n\nAlternatively, if Charlie is smart, but not quiet, that\'s allowed. There\'s no rule that says smart people must be quiet or anything.\n\nAlternatively, if Charlie is not smart, then he can\'t be quiet (since quiet → smart), so he must not be quiet. But that\'s okay.\n\nHmm, so in all scenarios, there\'s no contradiction, so the status of Charlie\'s bigness is not determined by the given premises. Therefore, the answer should be unknown, option C.\n\nWait, but let me see if there\'s a way to derive a contradiction in one of the cases.\n\nSuppose Charlie is big. Then he can\'t be blue (as explained earlier). So he must be a non-blue color, like maybe red or something. But being non-blue doesn\'t conflict with anything. All blue things are young, but non-blue things can be young or not? Wait, no, the premise says all blue things are young, but non-blue things can be any age. Since Charlie is young, that\'s okay.\n\nAlternatively, is there a rule that requires him to be blue? No. So that\'s possible.\n\nAlternatively, suppose Charlie is not big. Then he can be any color, and it\'s okay. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
