stated to be kind, maybe that\'s okay.\n\nWait, but if Bob is young, according to rule 3 (all young things are kind), so if Bob is young, then he must be kind. But Bob\'s given attributes don\'t mention him being kind. Wait, but if he is young, then he must be kind. But the problem is, if Bob is round (from being white), then he\'s young (from rule 4), so he must be kind (from rule 3). But Bob\'s attributes are only quiet and white. So does that mean Bob must be kind as well? Because if he is young, then he must be kind. But the original context didn\'t state that Bob is kind. Is that a problem?.\n\nHmm. Let\'s see. Let\'s track Bob\'s inferred attributes step by step:.\n\n1. Bob is quiet (given).\n2. Bob is white (given).\n3. Therefore, Bob is round (from rule 1).\n4. Therefore, Bob is young (from rule 4, because round → young).\n5. Therefore, Bob must be kind (from rule 3, since young → kind).\n\nBut the problem is, the original context didn\'t list Bob as being kind. However, according to the rules, if Bob is young (inferred from being round), then he must be kind. So does that mean Bob is kind, even though it wasn\'t listed? The problem states the given attributes, but the rules allow us to infer new attributes. So, Bob\'s attributes would now include kind, because of being young.\n\nTherefore, Bob is kind, quiet, white, round, and young. But the question is whether Bob is not young. Since we just inferred he is young, the statement "Bob is not young" is false. Therefore, the answer should be B) False.\n\nWait, but maybe there\'s an inconsistency here. Let me check again. Let me see if any rule contradicts this.\n\nLet\'s see other rules. For example, rule 5: All quiet, kind things are white. Bob is quiet and kind (if he is young, then kind), so he must be white, which he is. So that\'s consistent.\n\nRule 7: Young, quiet things are kind. Since Bob is young and quiet, then he must be kind. Which again, is consistent with our previous conclusion.\n\nRule 8: All quiet, red things are kind. Bob is not red (he\'s white), so that doesn\'t apply.\n\nRule 6: Quiet, green things are red. Bob isn\'t green (since he\'s white, and unless being kind and white would make him green? Let\'s check rule 2: If something is kind and white, then it is green. Wait, so if Bob is kind (as we inferred) and white, then he must be green. Wait, that\'s a problem!.\n\nHold on, rule 2 says: If something is kind and white, then it is green. So Bob is kind (from being young) and white, so he must be green. But Bob\'s attributes don\'t mention green, and there\'s no contradiction yet. Wait, but according to rule 2, that would require Bob to be green. But is that a problem?.\n\nWait, so Bob would have to be green. But is there any rule that conflicts with that? Let\'s see:.\n\nRule 6: Quiet, green things are red. Since Bob is quiet and green (from rule 2), then he must be red. But Bob is white, which is a contradiction because something can\'t be both white and red unless those are separate attributes (maybe they can have multiple colors? Wait, but in logic, maybe each attribute is separate. Wait, but in the context, for example, Dave is red and young, so colors can coexist with other attributes. However, the problem might be that being white and red at the same time is impossible? The problem doesn\'t state that colors are exclusive. Hmm.\n\nWait, but the problem\'s context gives some color attributes. For example, Dave is red and young. Fiona is red and round. Gary is red and quiet and round. Bob is white. So perhaps an object can have only one color? Or maybe they can have multiple colors. The problem doesn\'t specify. But in rule 6, if something is quiet and green, it must be red. So if Bob is green, then he must be red. But Bob is white. So unless being both red and white is allowed, this would be a contradiction.\n\nWait, this is a problem. Let me retrace:.\n\nIf Bob is kind (from being young), and white (given), then by rule 2, he must be green. So