is green (from rule 2). But Bob is white. Unless being white and red is possible. Since the problem didn\'t state that colors are exclusive, maybe that\'s allowed. Therefore, the conclusion is that Bob is indeed young, and the statement "Bob is not young" is false, so the answer is B. But the fact that he would have multiple colors might be a problem, but the problem\'s rules don\'t prohibit that.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made an error in applying the rules. Let me check again step by step.\n\nBob\'s attributes:.\n\n1. Quiet (given).\n2. White (given).\n\nInferences:.\n\n3. Round (from white → round, rule 1).\n4. Young (from round → young, rule 4).\n5. Kind (from young → kind, rule 3).\n6. Green (from kind ∧ white → green, rule 2).\n7. Red (from quiet ∧ green → red, rule 6).\n\nSo Bob is red, which is okay if multiple colors are allowed. So there\'s no contradiction unless the problem states that an object can\'t have multiple colors, which it doesn\'t. Therefore, Bob is indeed young, so the answer is B) False (because the statement claims he is not young, which would be false).\n\nWait, but let me check if any of the other rules might affect this. For instance, rule 5 says all quiet, kind things are white. Bob is quiet and kind (from steps 1 and 5), so he must be white, which he is. That\'s consistent.\n\nRule 7: Young, quiet things are kind. Bob is young and quiet, so he must be kind, which he is. Consistent.\n\nRule 8: All quiet, red things are kind. Bob is quiet and red (from step 7), so he must be kind, which he is. Okay.\n\nRule 9: If Gary is young then he is round. But Gary is already round (given), so that\'s okay. Not related to Bob.\n\nSo all the rules seem to hold if Bob is indeed young. Therefore, the conclusion is that Bob is young, so the statement "Bob is not young" is false, so the answer should be B) False.\n\nWait, but let me check if there\'s any possibility that Bob isn\'t young. Suppose that somehow Bob isn\'t young, but that would mean he isn\'t round (contradicting rule 1, since he\'s white and must be round, then round requires young). So the only way for Bob not to be young is if he isn\'t round, but he must be round because he\'s white. Therefore, Bob must be young. So the statement is false, so the answer is B.\n\nHmm, but wait a second. Let me think again about the color issue. If Bob must be red, but he\'s given as white, does that mean there\'s a contradiction? Let\'s see:.\n\nIf Bob is white and red, that might be a problem if the problem assumes that an object can have only one color. Since the problem didn\'t specify that, but in the given examples, like Dave is red and young, Fiona is red and round, etc., they have colors and other attributes. Maybe the colors are separate, like an object can have multiple colors. Alternatively, maybe "white" and "red" are mutually exclusive, but that\'s not stated.\n\nIf the problem allows multiple colors, then Bob being white, red, and green is okay. If not, then there\'s a contradiction, which would mean our initial assumption (Bob is young) is wrong. But since there\'s no rule saying colors are exclusive, I think we have to go with the inference that Bob is young, making the statement false. Therefore, the answer is B) False.\n\nBut wait, let me check another angle. Suppose someone says that "white things are round" (rule 1) and "round things are young" (rule 4). So white → round → young. So any white thing must be young. Since Bob is white, he must be young. Therefore, the statement that Bob is not young is false. So that\'s a straightforward path: white → young via the two rules. So even without considering the other attributes, Bob must be young. Therefore, the answer is B.\n\nI think that\'s the simplest way to see it. The other steps about being kind and green and red are extra but don\'t affect the conclusion because even if there\'s a contradiction in colors, the initial chain of white→round→young is solid. Unless there\'s a way for Bob to not be young, but given that he is white, he must be round, which requires him to be young. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
