all that, since there\'s no information about the bald eagle eating the bear, the statement "The bald eagle does not eat the bear" would be unknown. Because we can\'t confirm it either way. The premises don\'t mention eating by the bald eagle, so we can\'t say it\'s true or false. Therefore, the answer should be C) Unknown.\n\nWait, but wait a second. Let me check again. The question is whether the bald eagle does not eat the bear. So, to say it\'s false would mean that the bald eagle does eat the bear. To say it\'s true would mean it doesn\'t. Since there\'s no info, maybe it\'s unknown.\n\nAlternatively, could there be any inference that the bald eagle does eat the bear?.\n\nHmm, let\'s think. The bald eagle chases the cow and the bear. But is there a rule that chasing implies eating? The only rule that mentions eating is premise 11, where chasing the tiger leads to the tiger eating the bear. So perhaps chasing doesn\'t imply eating unless specified. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
