en, those are inferred, but they don't affect the big part. So the answer remains A.\n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is A) True. The user might have thought that because the problem didn't explicitly say Gary is big, but the rules enforce it. So the answer is A. But I'm a bit confused why the user thought B. Maybe they thought the roughness only applies to others? Or misapplied a rule. Anyway, logically, it should be A.\n</think>\n\nGary is rough. According to the rule \"Rough people are big\" (R \u2192 B), being rough necessitates being big. Since Gary is explicitly stated to be rough, this rule directly implies Gary must be big. No contradictions or exceptions to this rule are provided in the context. Thus, the statement \"Gary is big\" is **true**.\n\n**Answer:** A) True",
