ules and Gary's attributes step-by-step:\n\n1. **Gary's Given Attributes**:  \n   - Gary is **green** (explicitly stated).  \n   - Gary is **quiet** (explicitly stated).  \n\n2. **Applying Rules**:  \n   - **Rule 4**: \"If someone is quiet, then they are smart.\"  \n     Since Gary is quiet, he must be **smart**.  \n   - **Rule 5**: \"Smart people are big.\"  \n     Since Gary is smart, he must be **big**.  \n   - **Rule 6**: \"All big, green people are furry.\"  \n     Gary is now known to be **big** (from Rule 5) and **green** (given), so he must be **furry**.  \n   - **Rule 1**: \"All furry people are white.\"  \n     Since Gary is furry, he must be **white**.  \n\n3. **Consistency Check**:  \n   - **Rule 8**: \"Green people are smart.\"  \n     Gary being green already confirms he is smart, aligning with earlier deductions.  \n   - **Rule 3**: \"All white, big people are round.\"  \n     Gary is now white and big, so he must also be round (though this is not directly relevant to the question).  \n\nEvery step logically follows from the given rules, leaving no ambiguity. Thus, **Gary must be white**.\n\n**Answer**: A) True.",
