      "text": " B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe question is whether \"Bob is not blue\" is true, false, or unknown. Let\u2019s analyze the premises systematically:\n\n1. **Bob\u2019s Attributes**:  \n   - Bob is **nice** (given).  \n   - Bob is **white** (given).  \n   - Since Bob is white, by rule 10 (*If someone is white, they are quiet*), he is also **quiet**.  \n\n2. **Applying Rule 11**:  \n   - Rule 11 states: *If someone is nice and quiet, they are blue*.  \n   - Bob satisfies both conditions (nice + quiet), so **Bob must be blue**.  \n\n3. **Contradictions or Exceptions?**:  \n   - Rule 12 (*All white, furry people are blue*) only applies if Bob is furry, which we don\u2019t know. However, even if Bob were not furry, Rule 11 still requires him to be blue.  \n   - Rule 15 (*All cold, blue people are not furry*) only affects Bob if he is cold and blue. Since Bob would be blue (via Rule 11), he must also be smart (via Rule 8) and thus cold (via Rule 9). This makes him cold and blue, so he cannot be furry. But this doesn\u2019t negate his blueness\u2014it just restricts his fur status, which is irrelevant to the question.  \n\n4. **Conclusion**:  \n   - There is no premise contradicting Bob being blue. To the contrary, Rule 11 directly requires it. Thus, the statement \"Bob is not blue\" is **false**.  \n\n**Answer**: B) False.",
