ise8), then any entity that visits the lion must need the dog. For example, the bald eagle is visited by the lion (premise10), so the lion visits the bald eagle. Wait, so the lion visits the bald eagle, so in this case, the lion is the one doing the visiting. So premise21 would apply to the lion visiting the bald eagle? Wait, no. Let me parse it again.\n\nPremise21 says: If something (let's call it X) visits the lion (so X visits lion) and the lion needs the dog (which is true), then X needs the dog. So if X is the lion itself, then visiting the lion (the lion visiting itself?), but that's not relevant. The lion visits the dog (premise11), so when X is the lion, visiting the dog, but that's visiting the dog, not the lion. Wait, let's think of an example.\n\nSuppose the bald eagle visits the lion. Then since the lion needs the dog, the bald eagle would need the dog. But does the bald eagle visit the lion? Looking at the premises, the bald eagle's visits are to the rabbit (premise4). The lion visits the bald eagle (premise10), so the lion is visiting the bald eagle, not the bald eagle visiting the lion. So the bald eagle isn't visiting the lion, so premise21 doesn't apply to the bald eagle.\n\nTherefore, the only entity that visits the lion is the rabbit (premise14) and maybe others. Wait, the rabbit visits the lion (premise14), so applying premise21: since the rabbit visits the lion and the lion needs the dog, then the rabbit needs the dog. Which is indeed stated in premise9? Wait, premise9 says the lion needs the rabbit, not the rabbit needing the dog. Wait, premise9 is \"The lion needs the rabbit.\" So the rabbit needing the dog would be a new fact from applying premise21. Wait, let's do that:\n\nSince rabbit visits the lion (premise14) and the lion needs the dog (premise8), then by premise21, the rabbit (X) needs the dog. So that would mean the rabbit needs the dog, which is an additional fact not stated in the premises. But since we have to take the premises as g