ound.\n\n2. **Applying the Conditional Statement:**\n   - The statement **\"If Erin is red and not round, then Erin is nice\"** (Statement 11) is triggered because both conditions (Erin is red and not round) are true. Therefore, **Erin is nice**.\n\n3. **Nice \u2192 Smart:**\n   - **Nice people are smart** (Statement 14). Since Erin is nice, she must be **smart**.\n\n4. **Smart \u2192 Young:**\n   - **All smart people are young** (Statement 13). Since Erin is smart, she must be **young**.\n\n5. **Young \u2192 Furry:**\n   - **All young people are furry** (Statement 10). Since Erin is young, she must be **furry**.\n\n**Conclusion:** The logical chain (red \u2192 not round \u2192 nice \u2192 smart \u2192 young \u2192 furry) confirms Erin is furry. Thus, the statement **\"Erin is furry\" is True (Option A).** \n\n**Why Not Unknown?**  \nThe deductions follow strict logical implications with no gaps or ambiguities. Every step is necessitated by the given premises, leaving no room for uncertainty.",
