ng itself), and the bear visiting the squirrel? Do we know if the bear visits the squirrel? There\'s no fact stating that. The facts mention the squirrel visiting the tiger (fact7), the tiger visiting the squirrel (fact14), the tiger visiting the rabbit (fact13). The bear\'s actions are not mentioned except being cold, rough, etc. So the bear visiting the squirrel is unknown. .\n\nSo rule22\'s antecedent isn\'t met because we don\'t know if the bear visits the squirrel. So that might not help.\n\nAlternatively, let\'s think of other rules. Rule23 says if something eats the bear, then it chases the squirrel. If the bear eats itself (the bear), then the bear would have to chase the squirrel. But again, that\'s the bear, not the tiger. So that might not help with the tiger.\n\nHmm, let\'s look at the other rules. Rule19: If the rabbit chases the squirrel, then the squirrel eats the bear. But do we know if the rabbit chases the squirrel? The rabbit is cold (fact4), so by rule18, the rabbit would chase the rabbit? Wait, no. Rule18 says "if something is cold, then it chases the rabbit". Wait, the antecedent is "if something is cold", so the subject of the chasing is the cold thing. So the rabbit being cold (fact4) would mean that the rabbit chases the rabbit? That\'s another self-reference. Wait, no: "if something is cold, then it chases the rabbit". So "it" refers to the cold thing. So the rabbit, being cold, would chase the rabbit (itself). That\'s another possible conclusion. .\n\nSo if the rabbit is cold, then by rule18, the rabbit chases the rabbit. Then, according to rule19: if the rabbit chases the squirrel, then the squirrel eats the bear. But in this case, the rabbit is chasing itself, not the squirrel. So that doesn\'t trigger rule19. So rule19\'s antecedent isn\'t met unless the rabbit chases the squirrel. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a mistake here. Let me check again.\n\nRule18: If something is cold, then it chases the rabbit. The subject here is the cold thing. So if the rabbit is cold, then the rabbit chases the rabbit. That\'s a bit odd, but per the rule, that\'s the implication. Similarly, the bear is cold, so the bear chases the rabbit. The tiger is cold (fact10), so the tiger chases the rabbit. Wait, the tiger is cold, so by rule18, the tiger must chase the rabbit. And indeed, fact13 says the tiger visits the rabbit. Wait, but does it chase? The fact says "the tiger visits the rabbit", but rule18 says that being cold requires chasing the rabbit. So the tiger must both visit and chase the rabbit? Or does the visiting and chasing are separate actions? The problem doesn\'t say that actions are exclusive. So the tiger can both visit and chase the rabbit. So that\'s okay. .\n\nSo the tiger, being cold (fact10), must chase the rabbit (from rule18). So the tiger chases the rabbit. So that\'s an additional fact we can derive: the tiger chases the rabbit. .\n\nNow, since the tiger chases the rabbit, then by rule16: "If something chases the rabbit, then it eats the bear." Here, the "it" is the tiger, so the tiger must eat the bear. .\n\nAh! This is important. Because the tiger chases the rabbit (due to being cold), so rule16 says that the tiger must eat the bear. Therefore, the tiger eats the bear. .\n\nNow, if the tiger eats the bear, then according to rule23: "If something eats the bear, then it chases the squirrel." Here, "it" is the tiger, so the tiger must chase the squirrel. .\n\nTherefore, combining these steps:.\n\n1. Tiger is cold (fact10) → by rule18, tiger chases the rabbit.\n2. Tiger chases the rabbit → by rule16, tiger eats the bear.\n3. Tiger eats the bear → by rule23, tiger chases the squirrel.\n\nTherefore, the conclusion is that the tiger does chase the squirrel. So the answer should be A) True.\n\nWait, but let me check if there\'s any contradiction here. For example, rule9 says the tiger does not eat the rabbit. But in our deduction, the tiger eats the bear, which is allowed because rule9 only says it doesn\'t eat the rabbit. So that\'s okay.\n\nAnother check: The squirrel visits the tiger (fact7). The rule15 says if something is green and visits the squirrel, then the squirrel is green. But the bear isn\'t green (fact2), and the tiger\'s color isn\'t mentioned. So that doesn\'t affect.\n\nAlso, rule20: If something chases the bear and it (the something) visits the tiger, then tiger chases the squirrel. The tiger chases the bear (fact8) and the tiger visits the tiger? No, but the something here would be the tiger. The tiger does visit the squirrel (fact14), but not the tiger itself. Wait, the second part of the antecedent in rule20 is "it visits the tiger". The "it" is the something (the tiger). So the tiger would have to visit itself, which isn\'t stated, so that rule\'s antecedent isn\'t met. But since we already derived via rule23 that the tiger chases the squirrel, that\'s sufficient. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
