rrel visits the bear. Now, when the squirrel visits the bear, then by statement 17, the squirrel must see the bear. Additionally, by statement 18, the bear must see the dog. \n\nNow, the bear seeing the dog would then trigger statement 21 again, so the bear must visit the bear (itself). But that's acceptable. \n\nBut how does this relate to the lion? \n\nThe key point here is that the squirrel must visit the bear. But this doesn't directly affect the lion. \n\nWait, but the question is about the lion. Let me refocus. \n\nSo the squirrel must visit the bear because it sees the dog. But that's okay. \n\nNow, coming back to the lion's situation. The question is whether the lion does not visit the bear is true, false, or unknown. \n\nIf there's no information that forces the lion to visit or not visit, then it's unknown. But perhaps there's another angle.\n\nWait, but the dog visits the squirrel (statement 7). Since the dog visits the squirrel, and the squirrel is green, but that's okay. \n\nWait, the dog's visiting the squirrel doesn't trigger any of the conditions except maybe the squirrel's seeing the dog, which it already does. \n\nBut going back to the main point, the lion's visiting the bear: there's no information that forces it, but also nothing that prohibits it. So the answer is unknown. \n\nHowever, the user initially thought the answer was B) False, so perhaps I missed something. \n\nWait, let me check again the squirrel's visit to the bear. Since the squirrel must visit the bear (as per seeing the dog), then when the squirrel visits the bear, that's an action. But does that imply anything else about the lion? Not directly. \n\nAlternatively, suppose the lion does visit the bear. Then, as before, the bear must see the dog, which requires the bear to visit itself. That's allowed. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a different approach. Let's consider the contrapositive of statement 21 again. \n\nIf something does not visit the bear, then it does not see the dog. \n\nSo, if the lion does not visit the bear, then it must not see the dog. \n\nBut the dog sees the lion (statement 6). \n\nNow, if the lion does not see the dog, then the dog seeing the lion doesn't require mutual seeing. \n\nSo the lion could be not visiting the bear and not seeing the dog. \n\nAlternatively, the lion could be visiting the bear, which allows it to see the bear and the bear to see the dog. \n\nSo both possibilities are open, so the statement's truth value is unknown. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) Unknown. \n\nWait, but why did the user think it's B? Maybe I made a mistake in considering the squirrel's visit. \n\nWait, perhaps there's a way to show that the lion must not visit the bear? \n\nSuppose the lion were to visit the bear. Then by statement 17, it must see the bear. Then by statement 18, the bear must see the dog. \n\nNow, if the bear sees the dog, then by statement 21, the bear must visit the bear. \n\nAdditionally, if the bear visits itself, then by statement 17, it must see itself. \n\nBut then, when the bear visits itself, does that lead to any further requirements? \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the bear visiting itself is acceptable. \n\nBut is there any rule that would prevent the lion from visiting the bear? \n\nAlternatively, maybe the dog's age. Let's see. \n\nStatement 19: If something visits the dog and the dog is young, then the dog sees the bear. \n\nThe squirrel chases the dog (statement 12), but does the squirrel visit the dog? \n\nThe squirrel's actions: it chases the dog, chases the bear, is green, etc. \n\nThe squirrel visits the bear (due to seeing the dog via statement 21), but the squirrel doesn't visit the dog unless stated. \n\nThe dog visits the squirrel (statement 7). \n\nSo, the dog is visited by the squirrel? No, the dog visits the squirrel. \n\nSo if the dog is young, then if something visits the dog, then the dog must see the bear. \n\nBut if the dog is not young, this doesn't apply. \n\nThe dog's age is