statement 8), so it must eat itself. Then, by statement 10, since it eats the squirrel (itself), the squirrel must be green. Therefore, the squirrel is green. .\n\nSo the squirrel is red, blue, and green? That\'s okay unless there\'s a rule against it, but none is stated. So that\'s possible. .\n\nSo proceeding under that, the squirrel is green. Now, let\'s see if that affects anything else. .\n\nNow, going back to the lion. Let\'s see if there\'s a way to link the lion\'s roundness. .\n\nSuppose the lion is round. Then, per statement 8, it must eat the squirrel. But does that introduce any contradictions? The problem doesn\'t state anything about the lion\'s diet except that it doesn\'t eat the bald eagle (statement 5). So that\'s okay. .\n\nAlternatively, if the lion is not round, then we don\'t have to consider that. .\n\nIs there any other way to determine the lion\'s roundness? .\n\nLooking at statement 16: "If something visits the bald eagle and it is not round, then it visits the lion." .\n\nThe squirrel visits the bald eagle (statement 7). The squirrel is round (as established), so the "not round" condition isn\'t met, so the implication holds but doesn\'t require anything. .\n\nThe bear visits the lion (statement 4), so that\'s separate. .\n\nNow, let\'s think about the bald eagle. If the bald eagle is round, then by statement 11, it chases the lion. If it chases the lion, then by statement 14, it must be blue. .\n\nIs the bald eagle blue? Let\'s see. If the bald eagle is blue, then by statement 12, it must chase the squirrel, which it does (statement 1). So that\'s consistent. .\n\nSo the bald eagle could be blue and round. Let\'s see if that works. .\n\nIf the bald eagle is blue and round, then:.\n\n- Round → eats the squirrel (statement 8). So the bald eagle eats the squirrel. But statement 2 says the bald eagle eats the bear. So it can eat both. .\n\n- Round → since it\'s round, by statement 11, it chases the lion. .\n\n- Being blue, it chases the squirrel (statement 12), which it already does. .\n\nNow, if the bald eagle chases the lion (because it\'s round), then by statement 14, the bald eagle must be blue, which it is. .\n\nSo that\'s consistent. .\n\nNow, if the bald eagle is round, then the lion is being chased by the bald eagle. Does that affect the lion\'s properties? .\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s another path. Let\'s see if the lion is involved in any other statements. .\n\nThe bear visits the lion (statement 4). If the bear were to visit the lion and not be round, then by statement 16, it would have to visit the lion again? Wait, statement 16 says: "If something visits the bald eagle and it is not round, then it visits the lion." Wait, the bear visits the lion, not the bald eagle. So the bear\'s visit to the lion doesn\'t trigger that statement. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nAlternatively, let\'s think about the lion\'s possible color. Suppose the lion is blue. Then, by statement 12, it would have to chase the squirrel. Is there any info that says the lion chases the squirrel? Not directly, but if the bald eagle is chasing the lion (if round), then that\'s a different action. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the lion\'s color is not determined, but we need to see if its roundness can be inferred. .\n\nWait, let\'s see if there\'s a way to prove that the lion must be round or must not be. .\n\nSuppose the lion is round. Then, by statement 8, it must eat the squirrel. But there\'s no contradiction here. .\n\nAlternatively, suppose the lion is not round. Is there any rule that would require it to be round? .\n\nLooking at the statements, the only way something is round is if it\'s blue and red (statement 15) or if it\'s round via some other route. Wait, statement 15 says "Blue, red things are round." So that\'s a sufficient condition for being round: if something is both blue and red, it is round. But there might be other things that are round without being blue and red? The statements don\'t say that only blue and red things are round, so maybe other entities can be round for other reasons. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the only way to be round is through that condition. The problem doesn\'t specify, so we can\'t assume that. .\n\nHmm, so the lion could be round or not, unless there\'s another implication. .\n\nWait, let\'s think about the bald eagle\'s roundness again. Suppose the bald eagle is not round. Then statement 11\'s antecedent is false, so it doesn\'t have to chase the lion. .\n\nBut then, is there a way to determine the bald eagle\'s roundness? .\n\nAlternatively, let\'s see if the bald eagle must be round. .\n\nThe bald eagle eats the bear (statement 2). Since the bald eagle eats the bear, does that relate to any roundness? .\n\nWait, statement 8 says if something is round, then it eats the squirrel. The bald eagle eats the bear, but there\'s no statement that links eating the bear to being round. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a chain here. .\n\nWait, the squirrel is round, so it must eat itself (statement 8). That makes the squirrel an eater of itself, which then via statement 10 requires the squirrel to be green. So the squirrel is red, blue, and green. .\n\nNow, looking at statement 9: "If something is blue and green, then it chases the bald eagle." .\n\nThe squirrel is blue and green (since it\'s red → blue, and now green from statement 10). Therefore, the squirrel must chase the bald eagle (because it\'s blue and green). But statement 1 says the bald eagle chases the squirrel, not the other way around. Wait, that\'s a problem! .\n\nWait, this is a contradiction. Let me retrace. .\n\nIf the squirrel is blue and green (since it\'s red → blue, and by statement 10, since it eats itself (because it\'s round), then the squirrel must be green), then by statement 9, if something is blue and green, it chases the bald eagle. So the squirrel must chase the bald eagle. But the original statement 1 says "The bald eagle chases the squirrel." So the bald eagle chases the squirrel