ent 19, the cat must see the rabbit. .\n\nThe cat sees the rabbit. .\n\nNow, the cat is blue (statement 7). The rabbit is round (statement 12). .\n\nThe squirrel is big and rough (statements 15 and 16). .\n\nIs there any connection between the squirrel and the bear\'s needs? .\n\nWait, the squirrel eats the bear (statement 13). .\n\nIf the squirrel is needed by the bear for some reason? .\n\nWait, maybe the bear needs the squirrel to eat it? That doesn\'t make sense. .\n\nAlternatively, if the bear is eaten by the squirrel, maybe the bear needs to have the squirrel not eat it, but that\'s not a "need" in the problem\'s terms. .\n\nAlternatively, the fact that the squirrel eats the bear might mean the squirrel is a threat, but the problem\'s "needs" are about what the bear requires, not threats. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nAlternatively, the rabbit eats the squirrel (statement 9). The bear needs the rabbit. .\n\nSo the rabbit\'s survival might depend on the squirrel, so the bear needs the squirrel to survive so that the rabbit can survive. But again, this is assuming transitive needs, which isn\'t stated. .\n\nTherefore, without explicit info, it\'s unknown. .\n\nI think the safest answer is C) Unknown. .\n\nBut wait, let me check again the initial problem\'s conditionals for any that could require the bear to need the squirrel. .\n\nStatement 18: If something eats the rabbit, then it needs the squirrel. .\n\nNothing eats the rabbit, so this doesn\'t apply. .\n\nStatement 17: If something eats the rabbit, then the rabbit sees the bear. .\n\nSame issue. .\n\nStatement 22: If the bear is big and sees the squirrel, then it\'s round. .\n\nNo info on bear\'s size or seeing squirrel. .\n\nTherefore, there is no path that leads to the bear needing the squirrel. .\n\nTherefore, since there\'s no information that the bear needs the squirrel, the statement "The bear does not need the squirrel" is true. .\n\nTherefore, the answer is A) True. .\n\nBut why am I so conflicted? .\n\nThe key is 