tated, then that fact must be true.\n\nWait, in formal logic, the premises must be consistent. Let\'s see:.\n\nIf we have that the mouse chases the cat (from 11 and 16), then by 15, the cat must eat the mouse. Then by 12, the cat must be round. Since the problem doesn\'t state that the cat is round, but allows it to be, we can accept that the cat is round as a necessary conclusion. .\n\nTherefore, the cat is round (even though not stated), so the statement that the cat eats the mouse is true. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s another angle. Let me check statement 14. .\n\nStatement 14 says if something chases the cat, it doesn\'t chase the mouse. The mouse is chasing the cat (from above), so by 14, the mouse does not chase the mouse? Wait, that\'s not relevant. Wait, the mouse is chasing the cat, so the mouse (X = mouse) is chasing C, so according to 14, the mouse does not chase the mouse? Well, the mouse doesn\'t chase itself, which is trivial. Or perhaps it\'s saying the mouse doesn\'t chase the mouse, but that\'s not really an issue. Alternatively, maybe the statement applies to other chasers. .\n\nWait, the bald eagle chases the cat (statement 1). So applying statement 14 to the eagle: since B chases C, then B does not chase M. Which is okay, because there\'s no statement that the eagle chases the mouse. .\n\nBut the main point is that the mouse chasing the cat is okay, and that leads to the cat eating the mouse, which requires the cat to be round. Since the problem doesn\'t contradict that, we can accept it as true. .\n\nTherefore, the conclusion is that the cat does eat the mouse, so the answer is A) True.\n\nWait, but I\'m still a bit confused about the roundness of the cat. Since the problem doesn\'t state it, but the logic requires it, perhaps that\'s acceptable because the problem doesn\'t say the cat is not round. In logical reasoning, we can derive that the cat must be round in this scenario, even if it\'s not directly stated. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe 