, nice, and we inferred rough, hence young; Gary: rough, round, white, so young (from rule 8). .\n\nWait, is there a rule that connects white to being young? Let me think. The rules don\'t state that directly. White things are round, but roundness can come from other sources like being nice or being young and not nice.\n\nAlternatively, maybe Fiona\'s being white and round is sufficient for her to be young via some rule? Let me check again.\n\nWait, the only way to get round is through being nice (rule 1), being white (rule 2), or being young and not nice (rule 3). Fiona is white, so she\'s round via rule 2. So her roundness is due to being white, not necessarily due to being young or not.\n\nTherefore, since her roundness is explained by her being white, there\'s no requirement that she must be young or not young. So her age isn\'t determined by any of the given rules. Hence, it\'s unknown.\n\nWait, but let me check again for any possible paths.\n\nSuppose Fiona is not young. Is that possible? Let\'s see:.\n\nIf Fiona is not young, then she could still be white and round. There\'s no rule that says non-young things can\'t be white or round. The rules about young are more about when they must be round or smart, but Fiona doesn\'t have any conflicting attributes.\n\nAlternatively, is there a rule that requires Fiona to be young? Let\'s see:.\n\nNo, unless being white and round requires something else. But the only rules about being white are that they are round. So that\'s satisfied. So there\'s no rule that would force Fiona to be young.\n\nTherefore, Fiona\'s age is not determined by the given information, so the statement "Fiona is not young" would be unknown, because we can\'t confirm it\'s true or false. Wait, but let me check again.\n\nWait, maybe there\'s a way to infer something else. Let\'s see if Fiona is smart. The problem states that smart things are white (rule 6), so if Fiona is smart, then she must be white, which she is. But the converse isn\'t necessarily true. However, Anne is smart and green. Wait, but rule 6 says smart implies white, but Anne is green. Wait, that seems conflicting. Wait, Anne is green and smart. But rule 6 says smart things are white. That would mean Anne must be white, but the problem states Anne is green. That\'s a contradiction. Wait, that\'s a problem. Did I misread?.\n\nWait, let me check the problem again:.\n\nThe individual facts for Anne: "Anne is green. Anne is nice. Anne is not rough. Anne is smart." .\n\nAnd rule 6 says "Smart things are white." So if Anne is smart, she must be white. But the problem says she\'s green. That\'s a contradiction. Wait, that must be an error in the problem setup, but maybe I made a mistake.\n\nWait, maybe the problem says "Smart things are white" so Anne can\'t be both smart and green. That\'s a contradiction. Therefore, perhaps there\'s a mistake here, but maybe I misread the problem.\n\nWait, let me check again. The problem says:.\n\n"Anne is green. Anne is nice. Anne is not rough. Anne is smart. Bob is green. Bob is nice. Fiona is white. Gary is rough. Gary is round. Gary is white. If something is nice then it is round. If something is white then it is round. If something is young and not nice then it is round. If Bob is round and Bob is green then Bob is rough. If something is young and green then it is smart. Smart things are white. If something is rough and not green then it is white. If something is rough then it is young. All rough things are young.".\n\nAh, so Anne is smart and green, but according to rule 6, smart things must be white. Therefore, that would mean Anne is both green and white? But the problem doesn\'t state that. So that\'s a contradiction. Hmm, maybe that\'s an error in the problem, but perhaps I have to work with it as given. Alternatively, maybe I misapplied the rule.\n\nWait, perhaps "Smart things are white" is a rule, so Anne must be white because she\'s smart, but the problem says she\'s green. That\'s conflicting. So pe