ee the bear? The problem says the bear sees the bald eagle, but not necessarily the other way around. So the bald eagle does not necessarily see the bear. Therefore, the antecedent for statement 14 (seeing the bear) may not hold for the bald eagle, so the bald eagle might not need the tiger. .\n\nAlternatively, does the bear see the bald eagle, so the bear sees the bald eagle. But the bear is the one doing the seeing, so the bald eagle is not the one seeing the bear. Therefore, the bald eagle does not see the bear, so statement 14\'s antecedent is false for the bald eagle. .\n\nTherefore, the bald eagle doesn\'t need the tiger because of that. .\n\nWhat about the tiger? Does the tiger need the tiger? Not sure. But statement 17\'s antecedent requires an entity that both sees the rabbit and needs the tiger. Let\'s see if any entity does that. The bald eagle sees the rabbit (statement 6), but does it need the tiger? From statement 14, if something sees the bear, then it needs the tiger. Since the bald eagle doesn\'t see the bear (as established), then the bald eagle doesn\'t need the tiger. .\n\nThe rabbit: does the rabbit see the rabbit? Not sure. The rabbit is the subject needing the bald eagle and the bear. But does the rabbit see the rabbit? Not stated. .\n\nThe bear sees the bald eagle (statement 7). The bear also, does it see the rabbit? Not stated. .\n\nThe tiger sees the bald eagle (statement 12). The tiger chases the bear (statement 10). .\n\nSo perhaps the tiger sees the bald eagle but does it see the rabbit? Not stated. .\n\nTherefore, maybe no entity satisfies the antecedent of statement 17, so that implication is irrelevant here. .\n\nSo going back, the rabbit must be big because of statements 20 and 21. Therefore, the answer is B) False. .\n\nWait, but let me check another angle. Is there any rule that could prevent the rabbit from being big? Like, maybe being red and young or something else. But the rabbit\'s age isn\'t stated except that the bald eagle 