he rabbit. But the problem doesn\'t state that chasing requires seeing, so we can\'t assume that. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a chain of implications. Let me think again.\n\nWait, the squirrel likes the cow (fact12). Therefore, by statement18 (If someone likes the rabbit, then they like the cow). Wait, that\'s the other way around. Statement18 says if someone likes the rabbit, then they must like the cow. But the squirrel doesn\'t like the rabbit, so that doesn\'t apply here. .\n\nAlternatively, since the squirrel likes the cow, does that trigger any other conditionals? .\n\nStatement19: If someone likes the cow, they see the cow. Which we\'ve already used to conclude the squirrel sees the cow. .\n\nStatement18: If someone likes the rabbit, then they like the cow. So that\'s a different direction. .\n\nWhat about statement17: If someone is blue then they chase the cow. We don\'t know if the squirrel is blue, so that\'s not helpful here. .\n\nStatement16: squirrel is young, so doesn\'t see the dog. .\n\nStatement15: If someone is big, they like the rabbit. .\n\nStatement14: If someone likes the rabbit and doesn\'t see the squirrel, then they are big. .\n\nWait, the squirrel is chasing the rabbit. The dog likes the rabbit and the squirrel. Let\'s see for the dog. The dog likes the rabbit (fact5) and the squirrel (fact6). .\n\nDoes the dog see the squirrel? The dog sees the rabbit (fact7), but there\'s no info on seeing the squirrel. .\n\nWait, if the dog likes the squirrel (fact6), then by statement18, does that require anything? Statement18 is about liking the rabbit leading to liking the cow. So since the dog likes the squirrel, that doesn\'t trigger any conditional. .\n\nAlternatively, if the dog likes the squirrel, but that\'s not covered by the conditionals except maybe others. .\n\nWait, let\'s check if the dog is big. Suppose someone likes the rabbit and doesn\'t see the squirrel, then they are big (statement14). The dog likes the rabbit (fact5). Does th