f someone likes the cow, then they see the cow (statement19). We already have that. .\n\nIs there any other conditional that involves seeing the rabbit? .\n\nThe dog sees the rabbit (fact7), and the cow now sees the rabbit (from statement20\'s conclusion). .\n\nThe squirrel: does the squirrel see the rabbit? .\n\nSuppose the squirrel is chasing the rabbit (fact10). If chasing requires seeing, then yes. But the problem doesn\'t state that. However, maybe there\'s a conditional that links chasing and seeing. .\n\nLooking at the conditionals again, statement20 says if someone sees the cow and chases the squirrel, then they see the rabbit. But that\'s not about the squirrel chasing the rabbit. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s an implicit assumption that to chase something, you must see it. Since that\'s a common sense, but the problem\'s given conditionals might not require that. However, since this is a logic problem, we should rely only on the given statements. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a chain. Let\'s see:.\n\nThe squirrel chases the rabbit (fact10). .\n\nIf someone chases the rabbit, does that imply anything? .\n\nLooking at statement13: If someone chases the cow, then the cow is big. But that\'s about chasing the cow, not the rabbit. .\n\nStatement20 is the only one involving seeing the rabbit as a consequence. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the squirrel\'s chasing the rabbit implies it sees the rabbit, but since the problem doesn\'t state that, perhaps we can\'t assume. .\n\nWait, but the problem is asking if the statement "The squirrel does not see the rabbit" is true, false, or unknown. .\n\nSo, if there is any information that allows us to conclude that the squirrel does or does not see the rabbit, then it\'s either true or false. If there\'s no information, then it\'s unknown. .\n\nGiven that, let\'s see if any of the conditionals or facts lead us to that conclusion. .\n\nThe only possible path is through statement20, but the conclusion there was that th