nal 20: "If something is green and it needs the rabbit then the rabbit is green." .\n\nSince the rabbit is green, this is always true, so it doesn\'t give info. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nAnother thought: the rabbit visits the cat (fact 16). .\n\nIs there a conditional that says if something visits the cat, then something else happens, leading to needing the cat? .\n\nOnly conditional 17: if visits cat, then cat sees lion. Which we\'ve already used. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the fact that the cat needs the bear (fact 4) is relevant? Not sure. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the rabbit\'s visit to the cat implies that the cat sees the lion (from conditional 17). .\n\nBut that\'s already established. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the cat seeing the lion (from conditional 17) plus some other condition. .\n\nLooking at conditional 22: .\n\nIf (X sees cat) and (cat visits lion) → X is not rough. .\n\nWe know cat visits lion (from conditional 21), so the second part is true. .\n\nTherefore, if the rabbit sees the cat, then it must be not rough. But the rabbit is rough (fact 14), so this means the rabbit cannot see the cat. .\n\nTherefore, the rabbit does not see the cat. .\n\nTherefore, the rabbit\'s visit to the cat doesn\'t result in seeing the cat, which is okay. .\n\nBut how does this affect needing the cat? .\n\nHmm. .\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the rabbit needs the cat because of conditional 19 if it needed the lion. .\n\nBut without needing the lion, it doesn\'t. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the rabbit needs the lion because of another reason. .\n\nWait, the rabbit is visited by the lion (fact 12). Is there a conditional that says if the lion visits you, then you need the lion? .\n\nI don\'t see such a conditional. .\n\nConditional 18 is about visiting the lion, not being visited by the lion. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the rabbit is the "something" in another conditional. .\n\nLooking at conditional 20: "If something is green and it needs the rabbit then the rabbit is green." .\n\n