ear (fact 11). So if the bear were green, it would have to need the cow, but there\'s no fact stating that. So perhaps the bear can\'t be green, because that would require it to need the cow, but that\'s not stated. So maybe the bear isn\'t green.\n\nThe first conditional says if something is green, then it visits the tiger. If the tiger is green, then it would have to visit itself? Wait, the tiger\'s color isn\'t given except in the conditionals. The cow is blue (fact 3). The rabbit\'s color isn\'t stated except being cold and nice. So maybe the only green thing is the bear, but that causes problems. Alternatively, maybe nothing is green, so the first conditional doesn\'t apply.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bear\'s color is determined by the second conditional. Suppose there\'s an X that sees the bear and is cold. But since no such X exists (the rabbit is cold but doesn\'t see the bear), then the bear\'s color isn\'t forced by that rule. Therefore, the bear\'s color remains conflicting. This is a problem.\n\nHmm, this seems like a dead end. Maybe I need to consider that the initial color statements are all part of the premises, but they contradict, so the entire system is inconsistent. In that case, any statement could be derived, but since the cow visits the rabbit is given, maybe it\'s true? Or since the system is inconsistent, we can\'t determine anything, so the answer is unknown?.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem expects us to ignore the conflicting colors and just proceed with the given facts except the colors? Wait, but the conditionals involve colors. For example, the first conditional requires that if something is green, then it visits the tiger. So if the bear is green (as per one of the statements), then the bear must visit the tiger. But there\'s no such fact given. The bear\'s actions are only that it sees the rabbit. So if the bear is green, that would violate the first conditional unless it also visits the tiger, which isn\'t stated. Therefore, per