herefore, the bear cannot satisfy either condition to visit E. So the bear cannot visit E based on the given rules. Therefore, the statement "The bear visits the bald eagle" is false, because there\'s no possible way for it to happen under the given premises, and the premises don\'t state that the bear does so. Wait, but in logic, absence of information doesn\'t necessarily make it false. Unless the rules explicitly prevent it.\n\nWait, the problem is asking if it\'s true, false, or unknown. If the rules don\'t allow the bear to visit (i.e., there\'s no possible way for the bear to visit based on the given conditionals and stated facts), then it\'s impossible, hence false. Alternatively, if the rules don\'t prevent it but also don\'t require it, then it\'s unknown.\n\nHmm, tricky. Let me think again. The statements are all in the form of "if P then Q", which are sufficient conditions but not necessary. So the fact that the bear can\'t satisfy any of the given sufficient conditions to visit E doesn\'t mean it can\'t visit E for another reason not mentioned. So technically, the problem doesn\'t give us enough information to conclude either way, so the answer would be unknown (C). But wait, maybe there\'s a contradiction if we assume the bear does visit E, making it impossible?.\n\nLet me test that. Suppose the bear does visit E. Then, what would that imply?.\n\nIf B visits E, then from statement 14: If someone visits E, then E sees the dog. But E already sees the dog (statement 2), so that\'s okay. But does visiting E have any other implications?.\n\nAlso, if B visits E, we don\'t know B\'s attributes. Unless B\'s visitation would require certain conditions. Since the only ways to visit are via being kind or via being rough and liking the bear, but B can\'t satisfy those, then if B visits E, that would mean there\'s another way to visit not covered by the premises. Since the premises don\'t state that those are the only ways, it\'s possible. Therefore, the premises do