must be big. Therefore, the answer should be A) True. But let me check if there\'s any other constraints that might say something else.\n\nWait, the problem mentions "All quiet things are big" (statement 14). So if Gary is quiet, he must be big. Therefore, unless there\'s a way for Gary to not be quiet, but according to the previous steps, he must be quiet. Let me see:.\n\nGary is young → nice (statement 11). Then, all nice things are quiet (statement 13), so yes, he\'s quiet. So unless there\'s a way he can\'t be quiet, but there\'s no contradiction here. So he must be big. .\n\nWait another thought: The problem states "Young, quiet things are red" (statement 15). Since Gary is young and quiet, he must be red. But there\'s no mention of Gary\'s color in the context. But the question is about being big, so even if he has to be red, that doesn\'t conflict with being big. So that\'s okay. So the conclusion holds.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a loop here. Let me check all the rules again to ensure no contradictions in the logic.\n\nStarting with Gary\'s attributes:.\n\n1. Gary is young (given).\n2. Young → nice (statement 11). So nice.\n3. Nice → quiet (statement 13). Quiet.\n4. Quiet → big (statement 14). Therefore big.\n5. Young and quiet → red (statement 15). So Gary must be red. But the original context doesn\'t say Gary\'s color, but that\'s okay because the question is about being big, not red. .\n\nTherefore, based on the given rules, Gary must be big. So the answer should be True (A).\n\nWait a second. Let me check if there\'s any other rule that could affect this. For instance, statement 9: All nice, big things are white. If Gary is both nice and big (as per above), then he must be white. But statement 15 says he must be red. Wait, that\'s a problem! Because if Gary is both big and nice (from the steps), then he must be white (from 9), but also, being young and quiet (so red from 15). That would mean he has to be both white and red, which is a contradiction. .\n\nOh! This is a critical point I missed earlier. Let me retrace:.\n\nIf Gary is big (from quiet → big), then he is a nice (from being young) and big thing, so according to statement 9: All nice, big things are white. Therefore, Gary must be white. But statement 15 says that young, quiet things are red. Since Gary is young and quiet, he must be red. So now he must be both white and red? That\'s a contradiction. .\n\nHmm, this is a problem. The rules lead to a contradiction, meaning that maybe our initial assumption that Gary is big is wrong? But how?.\n\nWait, the contradiction arises because if Gary is big (from quiet → big), then he must be white (from nice and big → white), but also red (from young and quiet → red). So that\'s impossible. Therefore, there must be an error in the reasoning steps.\n\nWhere did I go wrong?.\n\nLet me start over again, more carefully.\n\nGary is young (given).\n\nYoung → nice (statement 11). So Gary is nice.\n\nAll nice things are quiet (statement 13). So Gary is quiet.\n\nAll quiet things are big (statement 14). So Gary is big.\n\nNow, since Gary is nice and big, he must be white (statement 9). .\n\nAdditionally, Gary is young and quiet, so he must be red (statement 15). .\n\nThus, Gary must be both white and red. But the context doesn\'t state that any object can be two colors. The problem doesn\'t mention that an object can\'t have multiple colors, but the rules lead to a contradiction. .\n\nThis suggests that there\'s an inconsistency in the premises, but the question is about whether Gary is big. Since the premises lead to a contradiction if we assume he\'s big (because he would have conflicting colors), maybe that means our initial assumption that he is big is invalid? .\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the contradiction means that the premises are inconsistent, but in terms of the question, we have to follow the rules as given and see what must be true.\n\nWait, but in logical reasoning, even if there\'s a contradiction in the p