d then she\'s just furry and young, with no further constraints. .\n\nTherefore, since there\'s no rule that forces Fiona to be smart, and no rule that forbids her from being smart, the status of her smartness is unknown. Therefore, the answer should be C) Unknown.\n\nWait, but let me check again for any possible constraints I missed. For example, could Fiona\'s youth have any impact?.\n\nStatement 14: Young things are not red. Fiona is young, so not red. That\'s already covered. .\n\nAnother thought: If Fiona is smart, then she must be big. If she\'s big, then she would have to be kind if she\'s also smart (from 8). But being kind requires her to be young, which she is, so that\'s okay. There\'s no conflict. But is there any rule that says she can\'t be big? The problem doesn\'t state that she\'s not big. So she could be big. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a rule that says if something is young and furry, it can\'t be big? I don\'t see any such rule. The only rules about big are from being furry and smart (15) or Bob\'s case. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
