ite\" is true, false, or unknown, we analyze the given premises step-by-step:\n\n1. **Key Properties of Gary**: Gary is cold (premise 6) and quiet (premise 7).\n2. **Rules Involving Whiteness**:\n   - Nice things are white (premise 11). Thus, if Gary were nice, he must be white.\n   - Rough and white things are quiet (premise 8). If Gary were both rough and white, he would need to be quiet, which aligns with his known quietness, but this does not confirm whiteness.\n3. **Coldness and Roughness**:\n   - Rough and nice things are cold (premise 12). If Gary were rough and nice, he would be cold, which he is. However, coldness could also arise from unstated reasons, so this does not force Gary to be rough or nice.\n   - All rough things are young (premise 14), and young things are round (premise 10). Thus, any rough thing must be round and nice (via premise 13: round and rough \u2192 nice). Therefore, **all rough things must also be nice and white** (since nice \u2192 white). However, this only applies if Gary is rough.\n4. **Scenario Analysis**:\n   - **If Gary is rough**: He must be nice (and thus white). This would make \"Gary is not white\" **false**.\n   - **If Gary is not rough**: His coldness and quietness could arise from other reasons, and his whiteness remains undetermined. He could be white (without being nice) or not white.\n5. **Conclusion**: Since there is no definitive evidence confirming or denying Gary\u2019s roughness, niceness, or whiteness, his whiteness cannot be determined. Thus, the statement \"Gary is not white\" is **unknown**.\n\n**Answer**: C) Unknown.",
