visits the cat, but not necessarily the squirrel. .\n\nTherefore, the only visitors to the squirrel might be someone else. Let\'s see if any other animal visits the squirrel. The cow? Not stated. The squirrel itself? Not sure. The squirrel\'s own actions? Not mentioned. .\n\nAlternatively, if the squirrel is big, then according to premise 19, it must visit the squirrel? Wait, no: premise 19 says "if something is big, then it visits the squirrel." So the big thing (say, the squirrel itself) would have to visit the squirrel. That would mean if the squirrel is big, then it visits itself. But that\'s a bit odd, but logically possible. However, that\'s a bit of a circular argument. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe another big animal visits the squirrel. For example, the lion is big (since it\'s nice, premise 6 and 18). So the lion is big. Therefore, the lion must visit the squirrel (from premise 19). Wait, yes! Because the lion is big (since it\'s nice), so according to premise 19, the lion must visit the squirrel. .\n\nAh! That\'s an important point. Let me clarify:.\n\nThe lion is nice (premise 6). Therefore, by premise 18, the lion is big. Since the lion is big, premise 19 says that the lion must visit the squirrel. So that gives us that the lion visits the squirrel. .\n\nTherefore, the lion visits the squirrel (from premise 19 via being big). .\n\nNow, with that in mind, let\'s see if that helps us determine the squirrel\'s bigness.\n\nWait, but we also had from premise 13 that the lion needs the squirrel, so the lion must visit the cat. Wait, but now the lion also visits the squirrel because it\'s big. So the lion has two visits: to the cat and to the squirrel. .\n\nNow, back to the squirrel\'s bigness. Let\'s see if the squirrel can be considered nice. .\n\nPremise 20: If something visits the cat and the cat visits the squirrel, then the squirrel is nice. .\n\nWe know that the lion visits the cat (from premise 13). But does the cat visit the squirrel? The cat visits the l