aybe there\'s an indirect way. Let\'s think again. .\n\nThe squirrel is young (premise 11). If something chases the squirrel and is young, then the squirrel needs the cat (premise 15). But unless there\'s a chaser, this doesn\'t apply. .\n\nAlternatively, the squirrel might chase someone. Let\'s see premise 17: If the squirrel visits the cat and the cat is red then the squirrel chases the cow. The cat\'s color isn\'t stated except the lion is red. The cat is nice, but not necessarily red. So unless the cat is red, this doesn\'t apply. Since the problem doesn\'t state the cat is red, we can\'t assume that. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nAlternatively, let\'s consider if the squirrel is big. Suppose it is big. Then, by premise 19, the squirrel must visit itself. Is that allowed? The problem doesn\'t prohibit self-visits. So that\'s possible. However, that doesn\'t give us any contradictions. .\n\nAlternatively, if the squirrel is big, then it is nice? No, because premise 18 is only one way. .\n\nWait, no. Wait, premise 18 says all nice things are big. It doesn\'t say that all big things are nice. So big things could be nice or not. So the squirrel could be big without being nice, but there\'s no information to confirm that. .\n\nTherefore, since there\'s no information that makes the squirrel big, we can\'t confirm it\'s big. But also, there\'s no information that says it\'s not big. So the answer would be unknown? .\n\nWait, but let me check again the premises to see if there\'s any other path. .\n\nWait, the squirrel\'s bigness is only affected by premise 18 and 19. .\n\nPremise 19 says big things must visit the squirrel. Wait, no: premise 19 says "if something is big, then it visits the squirrel." So big things have to visit the squirrel, but that doesn\'t mean the squirrel itself must be big. .\n\nWait, the squirrel\'s own bigness is only affected if it\'s nice (premise 18). Since it\'s not nice, it\'s not necessarily big. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the squirrel is visited by the lion (because the lion is big, so premise 19 requires the lion to visit the squirrel). But that doesn\'t make the squirrel big. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nSo, in conclusion: The squirrel is not necessarily big because there\'s no premise that makes it nice or another reason. However, the premises also don\'t state that it\'s not big. Therefore, the answer should be unknown (C). .\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Let\'s see if there\'s any way the squirrel is big. .\n\nWait, the lion is big. The lion visits the squirrel (because lion is big, premise 19). So the lion visits the squirrel. Now, does that affect the squirrel\'s bigness? No. .\n\nAlternatively, is there any other premise that could imply the squirrel is big? .\n\nLooking at premise 20 again. If the squirrel were to be nice, then it would be big. But to be nice, the squirrel needs that something visits the cat (which the lion does) and the cat visits the squirrel. Since the cat doesn\'t visit the squirrel, that condition isn\'t met. Therefore, the squirrel isn\'t nice, so not necessarily big. .\n\nTherefore, there\'s no way to confirm the squirrel is big. Thus, the statement "The squirrel is not big" could be true or false, but we can\'t determine. Hence, the answer is C) Unknown.\n\nWait, but hold on. Wait, let me see if the squirrel is not big, would that contradict any premises?.\n\nSuppose the squirrel is not big. Then, there\'s no issue with any premises. Because the premises only state conditions for big things to visit the squirrel, but the squirrel\'s own size isn\'t required elsewhere. .\n\nAlternatively, if the squirrel is big, that\'s also okay because it doesn\'t conflict with any given premises. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 55%|#####4    | 329/600 [4:38:56<6:31:41, 86.72s/it] 
