ot cold? Wait, the cat is cold (point2), but the lion\'s temperature isn\'t mentioned. Wait, the lion is rough (point10), but coldness? The lion\'s temperature is not stated. Wait, the problem says "the lion is not big" (point9), but nothing about the lion\'s coldness. So the lion\'s coldness is unknown? Hmm, so that part of statement18\'s condition (lion is not cold) can\'t be determined. So maybe that statement can\'t be used yet.\n\nStatement19: If something chases the lion, then the lion chases the cow. So if any entity chases the lion, then the lion chases the cow. Do we know if anyone is chasing the lion? Let\'s see. The lion chases the cat (point8), but does the cat or someone else chase the lion? Not mentioned unless inferred. Maybe the squirrel? Not sure yet.\n\nStatement20: If something chases the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the cat. So if someone is chasing the squirrel, then the squirrel will visit the cat. But do we know if someone is chasing the squirrel? Not directly. The lion is chasing the cat, but not the squirrel unless via statement17.\n\nStatement21: If the cow is rough, then the cow chases the cat. The cow\'s roughness? The lion is rough (point10), but the cow\'s roughness isn\'t stated. The cow is blue (point5). So unless the cow is also rough, this doesn\'t apply. Since the cow\'s roughness is not mentioned, we can\'t assume that, so this statement\'s antecedent is not met, so the consequence isn\'t necessarily true. So the cow might or might not be chasing the cat, but we can\'t confirm unless more info.\n\nNow, back to the main question: Does the squirrel like the cat?.\n\nWe know the squirrel likes the cow (point12). To find out if it likes the cat, perhaps we can look for any rules that connect liking the cat to other conditions.\n\nLooking at statement15 again: If something (X) likes the squirrel and is big, then the squirrel likes the lion. Let me see if that could be triggered. The only big entity is the cat (point1). Does the cat like the squirrel? The cat likes the cow (point3), but there\'s no mention of the cat liking the squirrel. So unless there\'s another entity that likes the squirrel and is big. The squirrel itself isn\'t big (we don\'t know, but the only big is the cat). So unless the cat likes the squirrel, then the cat would be X here, but since we don\'t know if the cat likes the squirrel, that\'s not helpful yet.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the squirrel\'s liking of the lion is required for some other condition, but not directly related to the cat.\n\nLooking at statement17 again: If something likes the cat, then it chases the squirrel. The lion likes the cat (because it visits the cat, so by statement14), so the lion must chase the squirrel. But the original point8 says the lion chases the cat, but statement17 adds that it must also chase the squirrel. So that\'s okay; the lion can chase both. But how does that help us?.\n\nIf the lion chases the squirrel, then according to statement19: If something chases the lion, then the lion chases the cow. Wait, but the lion is the one doing the chasing here. Wait, statement19 says if something (like X) chases the lion, then the lion chases the cow. But the lion is the one being chased in that scenario. Since the lion is currently chasing the cat and the squirrel (from statement17), unless someone else is chasing the lion, then the lion would have to chase the cow. But is there someone else chasing the lion?.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the squirrel is being chased by the lion, so if the squirrel chases the lion, then.. but that\'s not the case here. Hmm, maybe this path isn\'t helpful yet.\n\nLooking at statement20: If something chases the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the cat. Suppose that the lion is chasing the squirrel (from statement17, since the lion likes the cat), then that would mean the squirrel must visit the cat. So if the lion is indeed chasing the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the cat. But does the squirrel\'s visiting