(since he needs quiet and cold). So even though he's quiet, we don't know about coldness. Thus, he might or might not be white.\n\nTherefore, whether he is furry depends on being white and smart. Since he's smart, but white is unknown, so the answer is still C) Unknown.\n\nWait, but hold on. Wait, if all big people are quiet, then Fiona is quiet and cold, so she is white. Similarly, if someone else were both quiet and cold, they would be white. But for Gary, even though he's quiet, unless he is cold, he can't be white. His coldness is unknown. Thus, his status as white is unknown. Hence, whether he is furry is unknown.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be C) Unknown. But the user initially thought B) False. So perhaps I missed something?\n\nWait, another angle: Is there any rule that if someone is big, they can't be furry unless certain conditions?\n\nAlternatively, maybe Gary is not furry because of some contradiction?\n\nSuppose Gary is furry. Then he must be white (statement 10). To be furry, he must be white and smart (statement 8). He is smart, so he must be white. To be white, he needs to be quiet and cold. He is quiet, but is he cold?\n\nIf he is cold, then he is white, so he can be furry. But since his coldness is unknown, this possibility exists. Therefore, it's possible he is furry or not, so the answer is unknown. So C is correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's an implicit assumption that people are only the ones listed, but the problem doesn't state that. So perhaps the answer is indeed C.\n\nHmm, so I think the correct answer is C) Unknown. The user's initial thought of B might be incorrect. But let me check again.\n\nWait, let me check the statement 14 again: \"Big, smart people are quiet.\" So that's a universal statement. So if someone is both big and smart, they must be quiet. Since all big are smart (statement 15), then all big people are big and smart, so all big people are quiet. Therefore, Fiona is quiet (as big), and so is Bob (as big), Gary, Harry.\n\nTherefore, Fiona is quiet (from being big) and cold (given). Therefore, Fiona is quiet and cold \u2192 white (statement 11). So Fiona is white. Therefore, Fiona is white, smart (because big), so by statement 8, Fiona is furry. So Fiona is furry.\n\nNow, for Gary:\n\nIf Gary is cold, then he is quiet (from being big) and cold \u2192 white. Thus, he would be white and smart \u2192 furry.\n\nIf he's not cold, then he's not white, so can't be furry.\n\nSo whether he is furry depends on his coldness, which we don't know. So the statement \"Gary is furry\" could be true or false, hence unknown. So answer C.\n\nTherefore, the user might have made a mistake in thinking it's false. So I think the correct answer is C.\n</think>\n\nTo determine if Gary is furry, we analyze the given statements and inferential relationships step by step:\n\n### Key Inferences:\n1. **Big People**:\n   - All big people are smart (Statement 15: \"If someone is big then they are smart\").\n   - All big and smart people are quiet (Statement 14: \"Big, smart people are quiet\"). Since all big people are smart, **all big people are quiet**.\n   - Therefore, **Gary (being big) is smart and quiet**, as stated.\n\n2. **Fiona\u2019s Attributes**:\n   - Fiona is cold (given) and rough (given). \n   - Since Fiona is rough, she is big (Statement 9: \"All rough people are big\"). \n   - Fiona is also smart (as big) and quiet (as big). \n   - Fiona is **quiet and cold**, so by Statement 11 (\"If someone is quiet and cold then they are white\"), **Fiona is white**. \n   - Fiona is white and smart, so by Statement 8 (\"If someone is white and smart then they are furry\"), **Fiona is furry**.\n\n3. **Gary\u2019s Attributes**:\n   - Gary is big, quiet, and smart. \n   - To be furry, Gary must be white (Statement 10: \"All furry people are white\") and smart (Statement 8). Since he is smart, he only needs to be white. \n   - **Gary\u2019s whiteness depends on whether he is cold** (since being quiet and cold would make him white via Statement 11). \n   - **Gary\u2019s coldness is not stated or inferable**. The only explicit coldness is for Fiona (given) and Harry (via Statement 13). \n\n### Conclusion:\n- **Gary could be furry** if he is cold (and thus white), but his coldness is unknown. \n- **Gary could also not be furry** if he is not cold. \n\nSince there is insufficient information to confirm or deny Gary\u2019s coldness (and thus his whiteness), the statement \"Gary is furry\" is **unknown**.\n\n**Answer:** C) Unknown.",
