he bear is green. The bear is kind. The mouse needs the bear. The mouse visits the bear. The mouse visits the squirrel. The mouse visits the tiger. The squirrel chases the bear. The squirrel is cold. The squirrel is nice. The tiger is green. The tiger needs the bear.\n\nSo the mouse\'s attributes: only that it needs the bear and visits others. Not mentioned as kind, cold, etc. So the mouse isn\'t stated to be kind, so Rule 19\'s first condition (something is kind) wouldn\'t apply to the mouse visiting the tiger.\n\nHmm. Let me consider another approach. Let\'s see if there\'s any possible way the squirrel could chase the tiger through a chain of inferences.\n\nSuppose the squirrel needs the tiger (from Rule 14). Maybe needing the tiger implies that the squirrel must do something towards the tiger. But without a rule connecting need to chasing, that\'s not possible. Alternatively, if another entity is chasing the squirrel, which could trigger Rule 17, but that would require something else chasing the squirrel. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the tiger\'s actions? The tiger is green (fact 10), and needs the bear (fact 11). Are there any rules about the tiger\'s actions? Rule 19 says if something is kind and visits the tiger, then the tiger visits the squirrel. The bear is kind, so if the bear visited the tiger, the tiger would visit the squirrel. But the bear\'s actions aren\'t mentioned. .\n\nAlternatively, the mouse visits the tiger (fact 6). Since the mouse isn\'t kind, Rule 19 doesn\'t apply. .\n\nAlternatively, the tiger visiting the squirrel would be a separate fact, but that\'s not stated. .\n\nAnother thought: The question is whether the squirrel chases the tiger. Since the squirrel is nice (fact 9), and if something chases the squirrel (which we don\'t know if anyone does), then that something would have to be young (Rule 17). But that\'s not helpful here.\n\nWait, perhaps there\'s an indirect path. Let me consider all derived facts so far:.\n\nFrom Rule 14: squirr