herefore, the tiger needs the mouse. .\n\nNow, knowing that the tiger needs the mouse, can we apply any rules? .\n\nLooking at Rule 12 again: If something needs the mouse, it is kind. Since the tiger now needs the mouse, then the tiger is kind. .\n\nWait, the tiger\'s attributes: fact 10 says the tiger is green, but it\'s not stated to be kind. But according to Rule 12, since the tiger needs the mouse, the tiger must be kind. So now the tiger is kind. .\n\nSo now, the tiger is kind (from Rule 12 and the fact that tiger needs mouse). .\n\nBut how does that help us with the squirrel chasing the tiger? .\n\nAlternatively, let\'s see if the tiger visiting the squirrel (from Rule 19) can be connected to anything else. .\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there\'s a chain that leads to the squirrel chasing the tiger. .\n\nWait, the squirrel chases the bear (fact 7). The squirrel is nice (fact 9). The tiger is now kind (from above). .\n\nLooking at Rule 20: If the bear needs the squirrel and the bear visits the tiger, then the bear chases the tiger. .\n\nDo we know if the bear needs the squirrel? .\n\nThe bear\'s needs aren\'t mentioned except in the context of others needing it. The bear is needed by the mouse and the tiger (facts 3 and 11). The bear\'s own needs aren\'t stated. .\n\nBut maybe from other rules. Let\'s see:.\n\nRule 15: If something needs the squirrel and visits the mouse, then the mouse needs the squirrel. .\n\nIs there any X that needs the squirrel and visits the mouse? .\n\nThe mouse visits the mouse? No, the mouse visits the squirrel and tiger. Wait, the mouse visits the mouse? Not that I know of. .\n\nAlternatively, the tiger needs the mouse (from above), but does the tiger need the squirrel? Not that I know. .\n\nAlternatively, the bear needs the squirrel? Not stated. .\n\nHmm. Let\'s think again. .\n\nWe have:.\n\n- The tiger visits the squirrel (from Rule 19\'s conclusion).\n- The tiger needs the mouse (from Rule 16 and Rule 19\'s application).\n- The tiger