erived facts so far:.\n\n1. Squirrel needs tiger (from Rule 14).\n2. Mouse needs mouse (from Rule 16).\n3. Therefore, mouse is kind (from Rule 12).\n4. Since mouse is kind and visits the tiger (fact 6), by Rule 19, tiger visits the squirrel.\n5. Therefore, tiger needs the mouse (from Rule 16 applied to tiger visiting squirrel).\n6. Therefore, tiger is kind (from Rule 12).\n7. The bear is kind (fact 2). If the bear were to visit the tiger, then tiger would visit squirrel (but we already have that). But the bear\'s visit isn\'t mentioned.\n\nNow, with the tiger visiting the squirrel, can we infer anything else?.\n\nLooking at Rule 15 again. If something needs the squirrel and visits the mouse, then mouse needs the squirrel. .\n\nIs there an X that needs the squirrel and visits the mouse?.\n\nSuppose the tiger needs the squirrel? Not that we know. The tiger needs the mouse (from step 5), but not necessarily the squirrel. .\n\nAlternatively, the mouse needs the squirrel? Not unless from Rule 15\'s conclusion. Let\'s see:.\n\nIf X needs the squirrel and visits the mouse, then mouse needs the squirrel. .\n\nSuppose X is the mouse. Does the mouse need the squirrel? Not yet. But if the mouse visits the mouse (which it doesn\'t), but the mouse does visit the squirrel and tiger. Wait, the mouse visits the mouse? No. .\n\nAlternatively, if the tiger needs the squirrel and visits the mouse, then the mouse would need the squirrel. But the tiger doesn\'t visit the mouse (the mouse visits the tiger, but the tiger visiting the mouse isn\'t stated). .\n\nHmm. Maybe this path isn\'t helpful. .\n\nAlternatively, looking at Rule 17 again. If something chases the squirrel and the squirrel is nice, then it is young. .\n\nIf the tiger chases the squirrel (which we don\'t know), then the tiger would be young. But that\'s not helpful for our question. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the squirrel chases the tiger because it needs the tiger (from step 1) and there\'s a rule connecting needing to ch