ion visits the rabbit. Thus, any visitor of the lion (e.g., the bear or cat) must need the lion. However, this doesn\u2019t force the rabbit to visit the lion.\n- **Rule 5**: The cat visits the lion, but this does not imply the lion visits the cat (only that the cat visits the lion).\n- **Rule 6**: The rabbit\u2019s roughness means it cannot see the cat even if it visits, avoiding contradictions.\n\n### Rabbit\u2019s Actions:\n- The context explicitly states the rabbit visits the **cat**, not the lion.\n- There is **no rule** requiring the rabbit to visit the lion. The lion\u2019s visit to the rabbit (context) does not necessitate reciprocation.\n- No rule prohibits or forces the rabbit to visit the lion, leaving it **undetermined**.\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe statement \"The rabbit does not visit the lion\" cannot be definitively confirmed as true or false. Since the context and rules do not provide sufficient information to conclude either, the answer is **unknown**.\n\n**Answer**: C) Unknown.",
