e round. Furry people are rough.".\n\nOkay, so points 1-7 are individual attributes, and points 8-15 are general rules.\n\nNow the question is whether "Gary is young" is true, false, or unknown. The options are A (True), B (False), C (Unknown). But looking at the context, point 6 says "Gary is young." Wait, point 6 is "Gary is young." So that directly states that Gary is young. So why is this a question? Maybe I\'m missing something here?.\n\nWait, let me check again. Let me count the statements again carefully:.\n\nThe context starts with:.\n\nAnne is quiet. (1).\n\nBob is not rough. (2).\n\nGary is big. (3).\n\nGary is kind. (4).\n\nGary is rough. (5).\n\nGary is young. (6).\n\nHarry is young. (7).\n\nAll big people are furry. (8).\n\nYoung people are furry. (9).\n\nAll quiet, kind people are furry. (10).\n\nIf Harry is furry and Harry is quiet then Harry is round. (11).\n\nRough, kind people are quiet. (12).\n\nYoung, rough people are kind. (13).\n\nAll quiet, furry people are round. (14).\n\nFurry people are rough. (15).\n\nWait, so point 6 explicitly states "Gary is young." So according to the given information, Gary is young. Therefore, the answer should be A) True. But maybe there\'s a trick here? Let me see if there\'s any conflicting information.\n\nWait, perhaps the question is a trick because even though it\'s stated, maybe there\'s some contradiction in the rules that would invalidate it? Let me check the rules to see if there\'s any inconsistency with Gary being young.\n\nFirst, Gary is big (3), so by rule 8 (All big people are furry), Gary must be furry. Also, since Gary is young (6), rule 9 (Young people are furry) also makes him furry. So that\'s consistent.\n\nGary is rough (5), and since he\'s furry, rule 15 says "Furry people are rough." So that\'s okay because Gary is rough. So that\'s consistent.\n\nGary is kind (4). Now, let\'s look at rule 12: "Rough, kind people are quiet." Since Gary is both rough and kind, he should be quiet. But the first statement says Anne is quiet, but Gary\'s quietness isn\'t mentioned directly. Wait, does the context say anything about Gary\'s quietness?.\n\nWait, the context says:.\n\nGary is big, kind, rough, young. So his attributes are big, kind, rough, young. The only mention of quietness is Anne being quiet, Bob not being rough, and the rules.\n\nSo according to rule 12: "Rough, kind people are quiet." Since Gary is both rough and kind, he must be quiet. But the context doesn\'t explicitly state that Gary is quiet, but according to the rules, he must be. So that\'s an inferred attribute. But does that cause a problem?.\n\nWait, the problem\'s question is only about whether Gary is young, which is directly stated in the context. The question might be trying to see if there\'s a contradiction in his other attributes that would make the initial statement about him being young false, but I don\'t see that.\n\nWait, let me check another rule. Rule 13: "Young, rough people are kind." Gary is young and rough, so he must be kind, which he is. So that\'s okay.\n\nRule 14: "All quiet, furry people are round." If Gary is quiet (as per rule 12), and he\'s furry (from his being big and young), then he must be round. But the context doesn\'t mention Gary being round. However, that\'s okay because the question isn\'t about roundness. The question is just about his age.\n\nWait, but maybe there\'s an inconsistency here. Let me see:.\n\nGary is big → furry (rule 8).\n\nGary is young → furry (rule 9) → so he is furry.\n\nHe is rough, so rule 15 says furry people are rough, which he is, so that\'s okay.\n\nHe is rough and kind → must be quiet (rule 12). So he must be quiet. But the context doesn\'t say he is quiet. However, the problem is only asking about being young, which is given. So unless the rules somehow contradict the given information, but the given information includes "Gary is young" as a fact. So unless there\'s a rule that says young people can\'t be something else that Gary is