ald eagle. By the premise \"If something likes the bald eagle then it is young,\" the bear must be young.\n   - The squirrel is explicitly stated to be young.\n   - The tiger's age is not directly mentioned, and no premise directly links the tiger's age to any other attribute.\n\n2. **Key Rules**:\n   - The premise \"If something likes the bald eagle then it is young\" only applies to entities that like the bald eagle. The tiger does not like the bald eagle, so this rule does not apply to it.\n   - The tiger is green (due to being round, as per \"Round things are green\") but the rule \"If something is green then it needs the squirrel\" does not affect the tiger's age.\n   - The tiger's needs and relationships do not provide any information about its age through any of the given premises.\n\n3. **Conclusion**:\n   - The tiger's age is not determined by any of the premises provided. There is no rule that forces the tiger to be young or not young. Therefore, the statement \"The tiger is not young\" cannot be definitively determined as true or false.\n\nThe correct answer is **C) Unknown**.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
