y are furry (then they must be white). Since Dave isn't necessarily furry, he could be green without being white.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"Dave is not white\" could be true or false, so the answer is C) Unknown. But the user initially thought B (False). Maybe I made a mistake?\n\nWait wait, perhaps I missed a rule that connects green and white. Let me check rule 9 again: \"If someone is white and not green then they are round.\" The contrapositive would be: If someone is not round, then they are not (white and not green). Which means either they are not white, or they are green. So if someone is not round, they must be green or not white. But that doesn't directly affect Dave unless we have info about his roundness.\n\nAlternatively, if Dave is round, then? There's no rule that requires green people to be round unless they're kind (rule8). But unless he's kind, which requires him to be furry (rule2). Since we don't know if he's furry, then maybe he could be round or not.\n\nAlternatively, if someone is white and green, then are there any rules that would make them have to be round? Let's see: If they're kind, then yes (rule8). But they can be white and green without being kind (if not furry), so then they don't have to be round. So that's okay.\n\nHmm. So I think the conclusion is that it's unknown. Therefore, the correct answer is C.\n\nWait, but maybe I made a mistake here. Let me think again. Let's consider that all the attributes are binary (either they have it or not). Since Dave is green, could he be white?\n\nSuppose Dave is white. Then he must be big (rule4). He could be round or not, unless required. If he's kind, then he must be round. But to be kind, he must be furry (rule2). Since he's not mentioned as furry, he might not be. So no problem. Therefore, there's no contradiction.\n\nAlternatively, if he's not white, he could be non-white and green. There's no rule that says that's impossible. So the answer is unknown.\n\nWait, but the user thought B (F