e check again. .\n\nLooking at conditional G: If someone is round and eats the dog, they are green. Since the mouse isn\'t round, this doesn\'t apply. .\n\nThe only way for the mouse to be green is if some other condition applies, but there are no other conditionals that lead to being green except B\'s converse. .\n\nWait, the conditionals are only in one direction. For example, B says if green then likes tiger. But the converse isn\'t stated. So you can\'t infer that liking the tiger makes you green. .\n\nTherefore, the only way to be green is if some other condition (like G) is met, which the mouse can\'t satisfy, or by some other means not mentioned. .\n\nWait, unless there\'s an implicit assumption that the only way to be green is via the conditionals given. .\n\nWait, the problem only provides the conditionals listed. So unless there\'s a way to become green that\'s not mentioned, the only way is via G. Since the mouse can\'t satisfy G\'s conditions (needs to be round and eat the dog), then the mouse cannot be green. .\n\nAh! That\'s an important point. .\n\nBecause the problem only provides the conditionals given, there is no other way for an entity to be green except through the conditionals. Since the mouse can\'t satisfy the conditions in G (since it\'s not round and doesn\'t eat the dog), then there\'s no way for the mouse to be green. .\n\nTherefore, the mouse cannot be green. .\n\nWait, let me think again. .\n\nThe conditionals are statements like "if X then Y", but they don\'t say that Y can only happen if X. So the mouse could be green for some other reason not covered by the conditionals. But in logic, unless stated otherwise, we can\'t assume that the given conditionals are the only ways something can happen. .\n\nHowever, in typical logical reasoning problems like this, especially in exams, the conditionals are usually the only rules, so you can assume that the only way to be green is through the given conditionals. .\n\nTherefore, since the mouse can\'t meet the conditions for being green (G requires round and eats dog; the mouse isn\'t round and doesn\'t eat the dog), there\'s no way for the mouse to be green. .\n\nHence, the mouse is not green. .\n\nTherefore, the statement "the mouse is not green" is true. .\n\nWait, that changes things. .\n\nLet me clarify this point again. .\n\nThe problem\'s conditionals are all the rules provided. To determine if the mouse is green, we have to see if any of the conditionals\' antecedents are met that would necessitate the mouse being green. .\n\nThe only conditional that leads to someone being green is G: If someone is round and eats the dog, then they are green. .\n\nThe mouse is not round (the dog is), and does the mouse eat the dog? .\n\nLooking at the facts: .\n\nThe cow eats the dog (fact1), the dog eats the cow (fact7). The mouse\'s actions: the mouse is eaten by the cow (fact2), but there\'s no mention of the mouse eating the dog. .\n\nTherefore, the mouse doesn\'t eat the dog. .\n\nThus, the mouse cannot satisfy the antecedent of G, so there\'s no conditional that requires the mouse to be green. .\n\nTherefore, the mouse is not green. .\n\nTherefore, the statement "the mouse is not green" is true (A). .\n\nWait, but earlier I thought the mouse must be blue, but that\'s okay. .\n\nSo the answer is A) True. .\n\nBut let me confirm once more. .\n\nBecause the only way to be green is via conditional G. Since the mouse can\'t meet the conditions of G, there\'s no way the mouse can be green. Therefore, the mouse is not green. .\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A) True. .\n\nI think earlier confusion came from thinking that maybe being green could come from another path, but since there\'s no such path, the mouse can\'t be green. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
