ng itself, which might not be considered unless allowed. Since the problem doesn\'t mention self-visits, maybe that\'s an issue. So perhaps the rule in statement15 only applies when an entity visits both the squirrel and the lion, but not when it\'s the squirrel itself visiting itself. Therefore, maybe the squirrel can visit the lion without triggering statement15 because the "visits the squirrel" part would require someone else visiting the squirrel.\n\nWait, that\'s a good point. Let me parse statement15 again: "If something visits the squirrel and it visits the lion then it needs the squirrel." The "it" here refers to the same entity. So if the squirrel visits the squirrel (itself) and the lion, then it (the squirrel) needs the squirrel. But visiting oneself might not be considered a valid visit here. Since the problem doesn\'t mention any self-visits, perhaps we can assume that the squirrel doesn\'t visit itself, so visiting the lion would not trigger the first part of statement15\'s condition. Therefore, the squirrel could visit the lion without needing to satisfy the "visits the squirrel" part. Therefore, that might not be a problem.\n\nAlternatively, maybe "visits the squirrel" means visiting the squirrel entity, so if the squirrel is visiting the lion, then the "visits the squirrel" part would require another entity to have visited the squirrel. But that\'s not the case here. The squirrel visiting the lion would only involve the squirrel and the lion. So, in that case, the squirrel\'s visit to the lion wouldn\'t satisfy the condition of visiting both the squirrel and the lion unless someone else visited the squirrel.\n\nHmm, perhaps I\'m overcomplicating. Let me think of it this way: The problem gives us the following facts:.\n\nThe squirrel visits the bald eagle (statement10). The question is whether it visits the lion. Since there\'s no explicit information about the squirrel visiting the lion, and no rule that forces it to or prevents it, then the answer 