om statement15, since it would visit both the squirrel (if it\'s visiting itself) and the lion, then it needs the squirrel, which it does. But visiting itself might not be required, so that\'s okay.\n\nHmm. Since there\'s no explicit information that the squirrel does visit the lion, but also no rule that stops it, the correct answer is "unknown" (C). But I need to make sure I didn\'t miss any rule that would require it to not visit.\n\nWait, another thought: The squirrel is young (statement7). Statement17 says if something is young, it needs the tiger. The squirrel needs the tiger. Now, does needing the tiger imply that the squirrel must visit the tiger? Not necessarily. The problem doesn\'t state that needing requires visiting, so that\'s okay.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the squirrel can\'t visit the lion because the lion doesn\'t like the tiger (statement4), but that\'s about the lion\'s dislike, not the squirrel\'s visits. So that\'s irrelevant.\n\nHmm. Alternatively, let\'s think if there\'s any way to prove that the squirrel does not visit the lion. If we can find a contradiction if it did, then the answer would be "True" (A). But I don\'t see such a contradiction. .\n\nWait, let me try to suppose that the squirrel does visit the lion. Then, what would happen?.\n\n- Squirrel visits the bald eagle (given) and the lion (assumed).\n\n- From statement15: Since the squirrel visits both the squirrel (itself?) and the lion, then it needs the squirrel. But since it\'s already big (from visiting the bald eagle), it already needs the squirrel (from statement14). So that\'s okay.\n\n- From statement19: Since the squirrel is big and visits the lion, the lion needs the squirrel. There\'s no contradiction here because the problem doesn\'t state the lion\'s needs beyond needing the bald eagle. The lion could need both the bald eagle and the squirrel, so that\'s fine.\n\n- Also, the squirrel being green and young is okay.\n\n- The squirrel\'s other attributes (kind, young, likes