st visit itself. If neither can be proven, then it\'s unknown.\n\nIs there any conditional that would require the bald eagle to visit itself? I don\'t see any. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bald eagle\'s own attributes could be involved. For instance, the bald eagle is blue. The cow is also blue. The rabbit is cold. But there\'s no conditionals about being blue leading to visiting someone. .\n\nThe only conditionals related to color are statements 14 and 15. .\n\nStatement 14: If something is cold and visits the cow → cow sees cat. The rabbit is cold and visits the cow (as per earlier deductions), so the cow sees the cat. But that\'s a separate fact.\n\nStatement 15: If something is round and eats the bald eagle → sees the bald eagle. The rabbit eats the bald eagle (statement 7), but we don\'t know if the rabbit is round. So that doesn\'t apply.\n\nSo, coming back, there\'s no information that would force the bald eagle to visit itself or prevent it from doing so. Therefore, the statement "The bald eagle does not visit the bald eagle" is neither provably true nor false based on the given information. Therefore, the answer should be C) Unknown.\n\nWait, but maybe there\'s a hidden implication. Let me think again.\n\nWait, the question is about the bald eagle not visiting itself. So, to say it\'s false would mean it does visit itself. To say it\'s true would mean it does not. Since there\'s no info either way, but the problem might consider that since there\'s no statement that it does, it\'s unknown. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
