 likes the dog.\n15. The rabbit needs the dog.\n16. The rabbit needs the lion.\n\nThen there are several conditional statements:.\n\n- If someone chases the lion then they are round.\n- If the lion needs the rabbit and the rabbit chases the dog then the lion likes the dog.\n- If someone is round and they chase the lion then they need the cat.\n- If someone needs the cat and they chase the dog then they like the rabbit.\n- If someone chases the lion and the lion is blue then the lion is round.\n- If someone chases the rabbit then they are rough.\n- If someone is rough and they like the rabbit then the rabbit is young.\n- If the rabbit chases the cat and the cat needs the lion then the rabbit is young.\n- If someone is round and they need the cat then they chase the dog.\n\nThe question is about whether the lion does not like the cat. So, we need to check if there\'s any information that tells us whether the lion likes the cat or not.\n\nLet me start by looking at the direct statements first. The only mentions of "likes" are:.\n\n- The dog likes the cat (fact 8).\n- The rabbit likes the dog (fact 14).\n\nThere\'s no direct statement about the lion liking or disliking the cat. So, maybe we need to look at the conditional statements to see if any of them can lead us to conclude something about the lion\'s feelings towards the cat.\n\nLet me go through each conditional rule and see if they can give us any clues.\n\nFirst conditional: If someone chases the lion then they are round.\n\nLooking at the facts, both the cat (fact 1) and the rabbit (fact 11) chase the lion. So, according to this rule, both the cat and the rabbit must be round. Wait, but the problem doesn\'t explicitly state that the cat or rabbit are round. The cat is described as rough and young, and the rabbit is rough and blue. So, this condition would imply that both the cat and rabbit are round. But since the question is about the lion\'s feelings, maybe this is a side note.\n\nSecond conditional: If the lion needs the rabbit and the rabbit chases the dog then the lion likes the dog.\n\nHmm. Let\'s see if the premises here are true. The lion needs the rabbit? Wait, looking at the facts, the rabbit needs the lion (fact 16). The lion needing the rabbit isn\'t stated. The rabbit needs the lion, but the lion\'s need for the rabbit is not mentioned. So the first part "lion needs the rabbit" is not given. Also, does the rabbit chase the dog? The rabbit chases the lion (fact 11), but not the dog. So the second part of the condition (rabbit chases the dog) is false. Therefore, this conditional doesn\'t apply. So the conclusion (lion likes the dog) might not be triggered here. Not sure yet, but this doesn\'t directly relate to the lion\'s feelings towards the cat.\n\nThird conditional: If someone is round and they chase the lion then they need the cat.\n\nWe already know that both the cat and rabbit are round (from first conditional). Let\'s check each:.\n\nFor the cat: The cat chases the lion (fact 1). Since the cat is round (from first condition), then applying this rule, the cat needs the cat? Wait, no. The "they" here refers to the subject. Wait, the structure is "if someone is round and they chase the lion, then they need the cat." The "they" is the same as the someone. So, if the cat is round and chases the lion (which it does), then the cat needs the cat? But the cat already needs the lion and the rabbit (facts 4 and 5). The problem doesn\'t say the cat needs itself, so maybe this is a problem? Wait, maybe I\'m misapplying. Wait, the third conditional\'s conclusion is that "they need the cat"—so the subject (the cat in this case) needs the cat? That doesn\'t make sense. Wait, perhaps I made a mistake here. Let me re-express the conditional:.\n\nThird conditional: If (X is round AND X chases the lion), then X needs the cat.\n\nSo if X is the cat, then since the cat chases the lion and is round, the conclusion is the cat needs the cat. But that\'s not stated. However, in