ct? Wait, fact1 says the cat chases the lion. Hmm. Wait, the rabbit\'s age is not mentioned except maybe through the conditional. Let me see.\n\nThe seventh conditional says: if someone is rough and they like the rabbit, then the rabbit is young. .\n\nWho likes the rabbit? The rabbit likes the dog (fact14), but that\'s the rabbit\'s preference. The dog likes the cat (fact8). The lion\'s feelings aren\'t stated. Unless the lion likes the rabbit, but that\'s not given. So unless someone else likes the rabbit, this condition doesn\'t apply. Since the problem doesn\'t state that someone likes the rabbit (except maybe the rabbit likes the dog), this might not be triggered. So the rabbit\'s age might remain unknown unless another condition applies.\n\nEighth conditional: If the rabbit chases the cat and the cat needs the lion then the rabbit is young.\n\nThe rabbit chases the lion (fact11), but does it chase the cat? The problem says the cat chases the lion (fact1), but not that the rabbit chases the cat. So unless inferred, the rabbit doesn\'t chase the cat, so this condition\'s first part isn\'t met. The second part, the cat needs the lion (fact4), which is true. But since the first part is false (rabbit doesn\'t chase the cat), the conclusion doesn\'t apply. So this doesn\'t help.\n\nNinth conditional: If someone is round and they need the cat then they chase the dog.\n\nHmm. Let\'s see. First, who is round? From the first conditional, those who chase the lion (cat and rabbit) are round. Also, the fifth conditional made the lion round because it\'s blue and being chased. Wait, let me confirm that again. The fifth conditional says: If someone chases the lion AND the lion is blue, then the lion is round. Since the lion is blue (fact9), and both cat and rabbit are chasing it, then yes, the lion is round. So the lion is round.\n\nNow, who needs the cat? From the third conditional, the cat (who is round and chases the lion) must need the cat. Wait, but that\'s a bit strange