irectly).\n   - **Rule 9**: The rabbit (now inferred to need the cat and being round) must chase the dog (via Rule 9). This creates an inferred relationship where the rabbit chases the dog.\n   - **Rule 4**: The rabbit now chases the dog and needs the cat, so it must like the rabbit (self-reference, but no direct relevance to the lion).\n\n### **Analysis of the Lion\u2019s Feelings**:\n- **No direct or indirect rules link the lion\u2019s feelings toward the cat**. \n- The only mentions of \"likes\" involve the dog liking the cat and the rabbit liking the dog. The lion\u2019s preferences are never addressed in the premises or inferred through any conditional rule.\n\n### **Conclusion**:\nSince there is **no information** (either explicit or inferred via the rules) about whether the lion likes or dislikes the cat, the statement \"The lion does not like the cat\" cannot be definitively confirmed or denied. \n\n**Answer**: C) Unknown.",
