ybe there\'s another rule that requires kind things to be both furry and something else? Like maybe being round? Let me check all premises again.\n\nHmm, no. The only other way to be kind is being cold. So unless there\'s an implicit rule that all furry things are kind, which there isn\'t, then being furry doesn\'t guarantee being kind.\n\nTherefore, since we can\'t confirm whether Bob is furry or not, we can\'t determine if he is kind. Therefore, the statement "Bob is not kind" could be true or false, so the answer is unknown (C).\n\nWait, but maybe I\'m missing something here. Let me try to see if there\'s a way to deduce furrieness from other attributes.\n\nSuppose we assume that Bob is kind. Then he must be furry (premise 5). If he\'s furry, then by premise 8, he\'s green, which he is. So that\'s okay. But there\'s no contradiction here, so that\'s possible.\n\nAlternatively, suppose Bob is not kind. Then he could be furry or not. If he\'s not furry, that\'s okay. If he is furry, then there\'s no rule that requires him to be kind unless he\'s cold, which he isn\'t. So that\'s possible too.\n\nThus, there\'s no way to determine his status. Therefore, the answer must be C) Unknown.\n\nWait a second! Wait, let me check the premise 12 again: All round, cold things are rough. Bob is round but not cold, so that doesn\'t apply. Dave is rough, but that\'s separate.\n\nWait another thought: Since all kind things are furry, and all furry things are green. Therefore, kind things are a subset of green things. So Bob is green, so it\'s possible he is kind. But since there\'s no other rule forcing him to be kind, we can\'t assume that. So the statement "Bob is not kind" could be true or false. So it\'s unknown.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the fact that green things are round (premise 6), and round things are not quiet (premise 10), but that doesn\'t relate to kindness.\n\nHmm. I think I\'ve considered all angles here. I can\'t find any rule that would allow me to definitively say