und→green), and blue, perhaps there\'s a rule about green people? Let me check.\n\nLooking through the rules again:.\n\nRule14: Round → green. That\'s the only one about green except Fiona\'s not green.\n\nRule9: If Charlie is big, then ¬green. But Charlie is not big (since he\'s round and green, so if he were big, he couldn\'t be green, but he is green, so he\'s not big).\n\nHmm. Any other rules involving green? Not that I see.\n\nAlternatively, maybe Dave\'s greenness and being blue is okay. So no problem there.\n\nAnother angle: Let\'s see if being cold is linked to any attributes. The problem states that Anne is cold (fact1). The other mention of coldness is in rule8 (if Charlie is cold, then quiet) and rule13 (Dave\'s coldness if kind).\n\nBut there\'s no general rule that being a certain color or attribute makes you cold except those specific conditionals.\n\nSo, since Dave\'s coldness depends on him being kind, which we can\'t confirm or deny, then perhaps the answer is unknown?.\n\nWait, but maybe there\'s another way to find out Dave\'s coldness. Let me think again.\n\nWait, let\'s check all the people\'s attributes again systematically.\n\nStarting with Dave:.\n\n- Dave is blue (fact5).\n- Therefore, Dave is quiet (fact12: blue → quiet).\n- Therefore, Dave is round (fact15: quiet → round).\n- Therefore, Dave is green (fact14: round → green).\n\nSo Dave is blue, quiet, round, green.\n\nNow, could there be any other attributes? Let\'s see.\n\nIs there a rule that could lead to Dave being cold?.\n\nRule13 says if Dave is kind, then he is cold. But unless he is kind, we can\'t say.\n\nAlternatively, is there any other way Dave could be cold?.\n\nLooking at other rules, the only other cold reference is rule8 (if Charlie is cold, then quiet). But that\'s about Charlie, not Dave.\n\nSo unless there\'s an implicit assumption that only certain people are cold, but the problem only explicitly states Anne is cold, and the other conditionals.\n\nTherefore, unless Dave