gle sees the rabbit (statement6), and the bear sees the bald eagle (statement7). But there\'s no explicit statement that the bald eagle sees the bear. However, statement18 says that if something chases the rabbit, it sees the bald eagle. The bald eagle is the one chasing the rabbit, so applying that rule: the bald eagle must see the bald eagle (itself). That\'s a bit odd, but maybe acceptable. .\n\nBut for statement16\'s condition: if the bald eagle is big, then it must see the bear. Since we don\'t have any information that the bald eagle sees the bear, does that mean that the bald eagle cannot be big? That would create a contradiction. Wait, but that\'s a problem. .\n\nWait, so if the bald eagle is big, then according to statement16, it must see the bear. But there\'s no information that it does see the bear. However, the problem says "If something is big and it chases the rabbit then it sees the bear." So if the bald eagle is big and chases the rabbit (which it does), then it must see the bear. But since we don\'t have any statement that the bald eagle sees the bear, does that mean that our previous conclusion that the bald eagle is big is invalid?.\n\nAh, here\'s a conflict. Let\'s see:.\n\nIf we follow statements20 and21 to conclude that the bald eagle is big, then we must also satisfy statement16\'s condition. Since the bald eagle chases the rabbit, being big would require it to see the bear. But there\'s no evidence that the bald eagle sees the bear, so that would be a contradiction. Therefore, our earlier conclusion might be wrong.\n\nThis suggests that the assumption that the bald eagle is big leads to a contradiction because of statement16. Therefore, perhaps the bald eagle cannot be big?.\n\nWait, this is a problem. Let\'s retrace:.\n\n1. Bald eagle is red → needs the bear (statement21).\n\n2. Bald eagle is red and needs the bear → must be big (statement20).\n\nTherefore, bald eagle must be big.\n\nBut if it is big, then by statement16 (since it chases the rabbit), it must see the bear. However, there\'s no statement that the bald eagle sees the bear, so that would mean that the condition of statement16 is not met. Wait, but statement16 is a conditional: if (big ∧ chases rabbit) then (sees bear). So if the antecedent (big ∧ chases rabbit) is true, then the consequent must also be true. Since the bald eagle does chase the rabbit, if it\'s big, then the consequent must hold. But since we have no information that the bald eagle sees the bear, then the consequent is not known to be true. However, in logic, if the antecedent is true, the consequent must be true for the implication to hold. Therefore, if we have the antecedent (big and chases rabbit), then the consequent (sees bear) must be true. But if the consequent is not established, then the implication would be violated. Therefore, the only way for the implication to hold is that either the antecedent is false or the consequent is true. .\n\nTherefore, if the antecedent (bald eagle is big and chases the rabbit) is true, then the consequent (sees the bear) must be true. Since we don\'t have the consequent being true, the antecedent must be false. Therefore, the antecedent can\'t be true. Therefore, the bald eagle cannot be both big and chase the rabbit. Since it does chase the rabbit, it cannot be big. .\n\nTherefore, this creates a contradiction between the conclusions from statements20/21 and statement16. .\n\nSo let\'s resolve this contradiction step by step.\n\nFirst, from statements20 and21, the bald eagle must be big. But if it is big, then by statement16 and chasing the rabbit, it must see the bear. Since there is no statement that the bald eagle sees the bear, can we assume that it doesn\'t? Or is there a way for it to see the bear?.\n\nLooking at the given statements again:.\n\nThe only mentions of seeing are:.\n\n- bald eagle sees the rabbit (6).\n\n- bear sees the bald eagle (7).\n\n- tiger sees the bald eagle (12).\n\nThe bald eagle does not see the bear 