ecause of 21), then by 20, it must be big. .\n\nHowever, this leads to a contradiction via statement16. Therefore, the only way to resolve this is that the system is inconsistent, but since we have to choose between the options, perhaps we have to see which path is more direct. .\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made a mistake in assuming that the bald eagle needs the bear. Let me check again:.\n\nStatement21: "If something is red then it needs the bear." Since the bald eagle is red, that means it must need the bear. So that\'s a necessary conclusion. .\n\nStatement20: "If something is red and it needs the bear then it is big." Since the bald eagle is red and needs the bear (from 21), it must be big. .\n\nBut that leads to a contradiction via statement16. .\n\nHmm, so the problem might be designed such that the correct answer is "unknown" because of the contradiction, but that seems unlikely. Alternatively, maybe there\'s a way to resolve this without contradiction. .\n\nWait, maybe the bald eagle does see the bear, but that\'s not stated. Let me check the statements again for any mention of the bald eagle seeing the bear. .\n\nLooking at statement18 again: "If something chases the rabbit then it sees the bald eagle." Since the bald eagle chases the rabbit, then it must see the bald eagle (itself). That\'s a bit strange, but perhaps that\'s acceptable. However, that doesn\'t involve the bear. .\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the bear seeing the bald eagle (statement7) means that the bald eagle is visible to the bear, but not that the bald eagle sees the bear. .\n\nTherefore, there\'s no information that the bald eagle sees the bear, so the consequent of statement16 is false. Therefore, the antecedent (big and chases rabbit) must be false. Since it does chase the rabbit, it must not be big. Therefore, the bald eagle is not big. .\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) False.\n\nWait, but then why does statement20 and21 lead us to think it is big? The problem arises because the