ows for the bald eagle to see the bear even if it\'s not explicitly stated? Wait, no. The only facts are those listed. The conditional statements are rules, but the facts are the statements like "the bald eagle is green", etc. The question is based on the given information, so unless a fact is stated or can be inferred without contradiction, it can\'t be assumed. .\n\nTherefore, since seeing the bear by the bald eagle is not stated, and there\'s no rule that requires it unless being big, which creates a contradiction, the only way to resolve is that the bald eagle is not big, thereby avoiding the contradiction. .\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) False.\n\nWait, but that requires us to override the conclusion from statements20 and21. Which takes precedence? In logical systems, you can\'t have contradictions, so if following the rules leads to a contradiction, then the premises are inconsistent. But in this case, the question is asking based on the given information, so perhaps the answer is "unknown" because the system is inconsistent. But that\'s not one of the options. Wait, the options are A, B, or C (unknown). So C is an option. .\n\nHmm, this is tricky. Let me see if there\'s another angle.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the bald eagle\'s need for the bear is not a fact because of another rule. Wait, no, statement21 says if red then needs the bear. Since it\'s red, that\'s a must. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the "needs the bear" is not a necessary condition. Wait, no, the rules are logical implications. So the need is entailed. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bald eagle does see the bear, but it\'s not stated. For example, maybe through another rule. Let me check statement14 again: "If something sees the bear then it needs the tiger." .\n\nIf the bald eagle saw the bear, then by statement14, it would need the tiger. Is there any contradiction in that? Let\'s see:.\n\nIf the bald eagle sees the bear, then it must need the tiger (statement14). But does the bald eagle