ald eagle is red → needs the bear (from 21).\n\n2. Therefore, since it is red and needs the bear, it must be big (from20).\n\n3. Now, being big and chasing the rabbit (which it does), it must see the bear (from16). .\n\nThus, we can infer that the bald eagle sees the bear, even though it\'s not stated. Therefore, there\'s no contradiction because the system allows that inference. .\n\nTherefore, the bald eagle is big, so the answer is A) True.\n\nBut then why is the question asking if it\'s true, since it can be inferred through the rules? .\n\nAlternatively, the problem might be designed so that the correct answer is True, and the seeing the bear is an inferred fact. .\n\nSo, in that case, the answer is A. .\n\nHmm, now I\'m confused between A and B. Let me try to map it again step by step.\n\nSteps:.\n\n1. Bald eagle is red (given).\n\n2. By statement21 (If red → needs bear), so needs bear.\n\n3. By statement20 (If red ∧ needs bear → big), so is big.\n\n4. Now, being big and chasing the rabbit (given in 1), so by statement16 (If big ∧ chases rabbit → sees bear), so must see bear.\n\n5. Therefore, the bald eagle sees the bear is inferred, even though not stated.\n\nTherefore, all statements are satisfied. The only "missing" fact is that the bald eagle sees the bear, which we can derive from the rules. Therefore, there\'s no contradiction. Thus, the bald eagle must be big. .\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A) True.\n\nAh, okay, so my earlier confusion was thinking that we can\'t assume the bald eagle sees the bear, but in logic, if the rules require it, we must accept that as a necessary consequence. Therefore, the bald eagle must see the bear as a result of being big and chasing the rabbit. Since the rules allow that inference, there\'s no contradiction. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
