ermined regardless. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the error is in assuming that being white and nice leads to a problem. Let me retrace:.\n\nBob is white → quiet (rule 10). .\n\nBob is nice and quiet → blue (rule 11). .\n\nBut Bob is not blue (he\'s white), so the conclusion of rule 11 can\'t be true. Therefore, the antecedent (nice AND quiet) must be false. Since Bob is nice, quiet must be false. .\n\nTherefore, Bob is not quiet. .\n\nBut rule 10 says if Bob is white, he must be quiet. .\n\nSo there\'s a direct contradiction between the two. .\n\nIn logic, if you have two statements that contradict each other, the system is inconsistent, which means that any statement can be both true and false. But in this case, the question is asking about the truth of Bob being quiet. .\n\nIn such an inconsistent system, the answer is typically considered "unknown" because the premises contradict each other, making it impossible to determine a definitive truth value. .\n\nAlternatively, some might argue that since rule 10 is a direct implication from being white, and the other rules create a contradiction, but the question is asking based on the given information, perhaps we have to go with the direct implication. .\n\nWait, maybe the problem expects us to use the rules in a way that the contradiction is resolved by the other rules. Let me think again. .\n\nWait, another angle: Maybe the fact that all blue people are smart (rule 8), and if someone is smart they are cold (rule 9). Also, all cold blue people are not furry (rule 15). .\n\nBut Bob\'s color is white, so maybe those don\'t apply to him. .\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is designed such that the rules must hold, so we have to see which of the options is consistent with all premises. .\n\nLet me try to see if "Bob is not quiet" is possible. .\n\nIf Bob is not quiet, then rule 10 would be violated, since he\'s white. So that can\'t be. .\n\nAlternatively, if Bob is both white and blue, but the problem doesn\'t state that colors can\'t overlap. Wait, but the facts state that Bob is white and the rules don\'t mention that a person can have multiple colors. .\n\nWait, the problem states "Anne is cold. Anne is white. Bob is nice. Bob is white. Dave is blue. Gary is blue.." So each person has one or more attributes, but colors like white and blue are mutually exclusive? Or can someone be both?.\n\nThe problem says "All blue people are smart." So if someone is blue, they must be smart, but if someone is white, they can also be blue? The rules don\'t explicitly say that someone can\'t be both. .\n\nBut the problem says "All white, furry people are blue." That implies that a white person who is furry must be blue, but that doesn\'t mean they can\'t be white. .\n\nHmm, so maybe Bob can be both white and blue? But then the fact that Bob is white and nice would lead to him being blue (from rule 11 if he\'s quiet), but then he would be both, and that\'s allowed? .\n\nWait, but the problem says "All cold, blue people are not furry." But that doesn\'t affect Bob\'s color. .\n\nWait, but the initial facts state Bob is white. They don\'t say he\'s not blue. Maybe Bob is both white and blue? But then, being both would satisfy rule 11\'s conclusion (blue) even if he\'s nice and quiet. .\n\nWait, if Bob is both white and blue, then being white and nice and quiet would still satisfy the antecedent of rule 11 (since he\'s nice and quiet), but the conclusion is he must be blue, which he is. So that\'s okay. .\n\nBut can someone be both white and blue? The problem doesn\'t explicitly prohibit that. The rules only state implications, not exclusivity. .\n\nTherefore, if Bob is both white and blue, then there\'s no contradiction. .\n\nBut the problem states "Bob is white." It doesn\'t say he is not blue. So maybe Bob is both. .\n\nTherefore, if Bob is both white and blue, then being quiet (from rule 10) and nice (given) would lead to him needing to be blue, which he already is. So that\'s okay. .\n\nTher