*\n\n1. **Anne's Attributes**: From the context, Anne is explicitly stated to be **blue**, **rough**, and **young**.\n\n2. **Applying Rule 4**: \"All blue, rough things are red.\" Since Anne is both **blue** and **rough**, she must also be **red**. This directly contradicts the statement \"Anne is not red,\" making it false.\n\n3. **Checking for Contradictions**:\n   - **Rule 1**: \"All young things are white.\" Anne is **young**, so she must also be **white**.\n   - **Rule 7**: \"If something is young and red, then it is green.\" Since Anne is **young** and (by Rule 4) **red**, she must also be **green**.\n   - All other rules (e.g., \"All blue things are cold\" or \"All young, rough things are white\") are satisfied without conflict.\n\n4. **Conclusion**: There is no rule that negates Anne being red. Rule 4 mandates it, so the statement \"Anne is not red\" is **false**. \n\n**Answer:** B) False.",
