different color. Alternatively, maybe "Red people" (as in, people who are red) are green. So if an entity is red, then it is green? But that would mean red and green are compatible. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe "Red people are green" is a statement that all red entities are green, so red implies green. But then if the lion is red, then it would also be green? Let me note that. .\n\nSo the lion is red because it\'s eaten by squirrel and cat. If "Red people are green" applies, then the lion, being red, must also be green. .\n\nWait, so lion is red and green? That\'s possible. Then moving on. .\n\nContinuing, next conditional: If someone likes the lion then they visit the bear. .\n\nSo if an entity likes the lion, then they must visit the bear. .\n\nWho likes the lion? The lion likes the cat (point 6), so the lion likes someone else, but does anyone else like the lion? The squirrel? The squirrel\'s likes are towards the bear (point 11). The bear\'s likes? Not stated, except possibly through the third conditional. Wait, from the third conditional: since the lion visits the bear (point 7), then the bear must like the lion (from rule 16: If someone visits the bear, then the bear likes the lion). So the bear likes the lion. Therefore, the bear likes the lion. .\n\nTherefore, the bear likes the lion, so according to rule 19 (If someone likes the lion then they visit the bear), the bear must visit the bear? Wait, no. Wait, the rule says if X likes the lion, then X visits the bear. The bear likes the lion (from rule 16), so X is the bear. Therefore, the bear must visit the bear? That seems odd. But maybe that\'s a problem. Wait, the bear visiting itself? The original context says the bear visits the lion (point 4). But according to this rule, since the bear likes the lion (from rule 16), the bear must visit the bear. But that\'s a contradiction because the bear can\'t visit itself? Or maybe the rules allow it. Hmm, this might be an issue. Let me check again.\n\nWait, the rule 16