ed a step.\n\nWait, let me check premise11 again. The exact wording is: \"If something is big then it chases the lion.\" So, the subject (big thing) chases the lion (the object). So, if the lion is big, then it must chase the lion (object). That's self-reference, but unless prohibited, it's allowed. So, the conclusion is valid.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's a misinterpretation of the rules. Let me see if \"the lion needs the rabbit\" (premise3) has any effect here. The need is separate from chasing, so no impact.\n\nWait, let me recheck the initial problem statement to ensure I didn't misread anything.\n\nLooking back at the premises:\n\nPremise11: \"If something is big then it chases the lion.\" So, big \u2192 chases lion.\n\nPremise8: \"If something chases the rabbit then it is big.\" chases rabbit \u2192 big.\n\nPremise14: \"If something chases the lion then the lion chases the rabbit.\" chases lion \u2192 lion chases rabbit.\n\nThe chain is:.\n\n- Bald Eagle chases rabbit \u2192 big (premise8).\n- \u2192 chases lion (premise11).\n- \u2192 lion chases rabbit (premise14).\n- lion chases rabbit \u2192 lion is big (premise8).\n- lion is big \u2192 lion chases lion (premise11).\n\nThis is a valid chain with no contradictions. So the conclusion must be that the lion chases itself. So the answer is A) True. But the user initially thought B) False. Hmm.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the first step? Let me check again.\n\nWait, the problem says \"the rabbit needs the lion\" (premise6), but does that affect anything? Not directly.\n\nWait, perhaps there's a step where the lion can't chase itself because of another rule. Let me check all the premises again for any that could negate it.\n\nPremise4: \"The rabbit does not chase the lion.\" Doesn't affect the lion.\n\nPremise13: If something visits the tiger \u2192 tiger is not green. Not related.\n\nPremise16: If something chases the rabbit and doesn't visit the lion \u2192 lion doesn't need rabbit. We used this t