ear, then the bear would like the lion. But in the facts, does the squirrel visit the bear? The squirrel visits the lion (statement 12), but not the bear. The lion visits the bear (statement 7). .\n\nSo when the lion visits the bear (statement 7), then according to rule 16, the bear must like the lion. Wait, does that mean that the bear\'s liking of the lion is now established? Because the lion (X) is visiting the bear, so applying rule 16: If X (lion) visits the bear → the bear likes the lion. .\n\nTherefore, from statement 7 (the lion visits the bear), via rule 16, we can conclude that the bear likes the lion. .\n\nSo the bear likes the lion is now a derived fact. .\n\nIs that important? Maybe for other rules. Let me note that: Bear likes the lion.\n\nNow, looking at the squirrel again. The squirrel likes the bear (statement 11). .\n\nRule 19: If someone likes the lion, they visit the bear. .\n\nDoes the squirrel like the lion? Not directly stated. The squirrel\'s likes are only the bear (statement 11). So unless there\'s another inference, the squirrel doesn\'t like the lion, so rule 19 doesn\'t apply to them. .\n\nBack to the main question: does the squirrel eat the squirrel? .\n\nThe direct facts don\'t mention that. Let\'s see if any rules can lead us there. .\n\nSuppose we consider the squirrel\'s color. If the squirrel is green, then by rule 18, they would eat the squirrel (themselves). So to see if the squirrel is green, we need to see if any rules make them green. .\n\nThe squirrel\'s color isn\'t stated directly, except perhaps through other rules. .\n\nLooking at statement 13: "Red people are green." But the squirrel isn\'t a person, so that doesn\'t apply. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the squirrel is a person? The problem mentions "Red people are green," but the other entities (bear, squirrel, etc.) are animals, so probably not. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the squirrel\'s actions can lead to being green. .\n\nWait, the squirrel eats the lion (statement 8). F