lion. Let\'s see. The dog eats the cat (statement 11), so the dog is the one that eats the cat. The cat\'s eating: does the cat eat the dog? Statement 11 says the dog eats the cat, so the cat doesn\'t eat the dog. So the antecedent here requires two conditions: the eater of the cat (which is the dog) and the cat eating the dog. Since the cat does not eat the dog, the antecedent is false, so the implication holds but we can\'t derive anything about the cat eating the lion.\n\nStatement 21: If something visits the dog and it eats the lion, then the lion is round. Let\'s parse this. The structure is: If (X visits the dog AND X eats the lion), then the lion is round. .\n\nWait, the wording is a bit ambiguous. The original says: "If something visits the dog and it eats the lion then the lion is round." So the "it" probably refers to the same something. So the antecedent is: there exists something that both visits the dog and eats the lion. Then, the conclusion is the lion is round.\n\nSo let\'s see if there\'s such an entity. .\n\nFirst, who visits the dog? The bald eagle visits the dog (statement 4), the cat visits the dog (statement 10). Also, the dog itself doesn\'t visit itself, probably. So the bald eagle and the cat both visit the dog.\n\nNow, does any of them eat the lion? .\n\nFrom earlier, the dog is big (statement 12), so by statement 16, since the dog is big, it must eat the lion. Therefore, the dog eats the lion. Additionally, the dog also eats the cat (statement 11). So the dog eats both the cat and the lion. .\n\nTherefore, the dog is the "something" that visits the dog? Wait, no. The dog can\'t visit itself, I think. Wait, the dog is visiting someone else? The statements about visiting: the bald eagle visits the dog (statement 4), the cat visits the dog (statement 10). The dog\'s own actions: the dog eats the cat and the lion (from statement 11 and the implication from statement 16). .\n\nSo, the dog does not visit the dog. The entities that visit the dog 