d chases the bald eagle (which they are), then the bald eagle must be green. But the bald eagle is stated to be blue. That\'s a contradiction. Therefore, maybe the problem has an inconsistency?.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made a mistake. Let me recheck:.\n\nThe bald eagle is blue. The squirrel is red and chases the bald eagle. So according to the first conditional, the bald eagle must be green. But the bald eagle is blue. That\'s a contradiction. That would mean the given data is inconsistent, but perhaps we have to proceed despite that, assuming that the statements are all true? Or maybe I misread the conditionals?.\n\nWait, maybe I misapplied the first conditional. Let me re-express it. The first conditional says: If someone (X) is red and X chases the bald eagle, then the bald eagle is green. So in this case, the squirrel is X. So yes, that applies. Therefore, according to the conditional, the bald eagle must be green, but it\'s stated as blue. That\'s conflicting. So perhaps the problem is designed to have such contradictions, but the user expects us to proceed as per the given statements, perhaps assuming that the conditionals are part of the premises that must hold, so maybe the bald eagle must actually be green, overriding the initial statement?.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bald eagle being blue is a given fact, and the conditional is a rule that must be followed. But if the facts contradict the rule, perhaps the conclusion is that the premise is impossible? But the question is about whether the bald eagle chases the lion. Maybe this contradiction affects the answer somehow?.\n\nHmm, this is a problem. Let me see if I can resolve this. Since the problem states "given the above information," perhaps we have to take all the statements as true, including the conditionals, so the bald eagle must be green and blue at the same time? That can\'t be. Therefore, perhaps there\'s an error in the problem setup, but since we have to proceed, maybe we have to see if the 