y other condition except maybe if the mouse is rough. .\n\nBut we already established the mouse isn\'t rough. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nGiven all this, I think the correct answer is C) Unknown, because the information doesn\'t determine whether the mouse sees the squirrel or not. .\n\nWait, but wait! Let me check one more time. .\n\nThe squirrel is red (from being rough and seeing the dog), so squirrel is red. .\n\nThe mouse sees the cat (10), but the cat is also red. .\n\nIs there any rule that requires something seeing a red thing? .\n\nStatement 13 says if you see the dog, you must be red. .\n\nBut seeing other red things doesn\'t have a rule. .\n\nTherefore, the mouse seeing the squirrel (who is red) doesn\'t violate any rules, but also isn\'t required. .\n\nTherefore, no conclusion can be drawn, so the answer is unknown. .\n\nBut wait, the problem says "if the cat likes the mouse then the mouse sees the squirrel." .\n\nBut the contrapositive is "if the mouse does not see the squirrel, then the cat does not like the mouse." .\n\nThe question is asking whether the mouse does not see the squirrel. .\n\nIf that were true, then the cat does not like the mouse. .\n\nBut the problem states the cat does not like the squirrel (3), but there\'s no contradiction in the cat not liking the mouse. .\n\nTherefore, it\'s possible for the mouse to not see the squirrel (so the statement is true), or to see it (so the statement is false). .\n\nHence, it\'s unknown. .\n\nTherefore, the answer should be C) Unknown. .\n\nWait, but I just realized something. .\n\nThe mouse needs the cat (from 9) and likes the cat (8). .\n\nThe dog needs both the cat and the mouse (from 6 and 15). .\n\nThe dog must like the cat (from 18). .\n\nIs there any connection between the dog liking the cat and the mouse\'s seeing the squirrel? .\n\nI don\'t see it. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the mouse must see the squirrel because the squirrel is young and sees the dog, but that doesn\'t imply anything about the mouse. .\