rrel must be cold. \n\nTherefore, squirrel is cold. \n\nNow, since the squirrel is cold, conditional5 requires that the squirrel must like the mouse (since the squirrel is cold). \n\nSo squirrel likes the mouse. \n\nNow, the squirrel likes the mouse and the mouse visits the lion (fact5). So applying conditional6 again with X = squirrel:\n\nIf (squirrel likes mouse) AND (mouse visits lion) \u2192 squirrel must like the squirrel. \n\nThus, the squirrel must like itself, which is okay. \n\nNow, is there any rule that would cause the lion to like the mouse? \n\nSuppose the lion is the one who likes the mouse. Then, conditional6 requires the lion to like the squirrel. \n\nThe lion eating the squirrel isn't a problem unless there's a rule that says if you eat someone you can't like them, but there isn't. \n\nAlternatively, the lion could be cold? If the lion were cold, then conditional5 would require the lion to like the mouse. Wait, that's an important point!\n\nIf the lion were cold, then conditional5 says the lion must like the mouse. \n\nBut we don't know if the lion is cold. The only cold entity stated is the mouse. However, the squirrel is now cold because of the mouse's liking. \n\nWait, the squirrel became cold because the mouse (who is cold) likes the squirrel. \n\nNow, the squirrel is cold. Therefore, if the lion were to be cold, it would have to like the mouse. But the problem doesn't say the lion is cold. \n\nThe key is that there's no information about the lion's temperature. Therefore, it could be cold or not. \n\nIf the lion is not cold, then it doesn't have to like the mouse (conditional5 doesn't apply). \n\nIf the lion is cold, then it must like the mouse (conditional5). \n\nBut since we don't know the lion's temperature, we can't determine. \n\nHowever, the problem states \"Given the context...\" which includes the facts and conditionals. The facts don't mention the lion's temperature. So we can't assume it's cold unless inferred. \n\nIs there any way to