 The statement to evaluate is "Gary is not cold." So we need to determine if we can conclude that Gary is cold, not cold, or it\'s unknown.\n\nFirst, let\'s look at what\'s known about Gary from the context:.\n\nGary is kind, Gary is nice, Gary is red.\n\nNow, let\'s recall the general rules:.\n\n1. Kind things are cold. So if something is kind, it must be cold. Therefore, since Gary is kind, according to this rule, Gary must be cold. Wait, but that would mean Gary is cold, which would make the statement "Gary is not cold" false. But the options are A) True (so the statement is true, meaning Gary is not cold), B) False (statement is false, meaning Gary is cold), or C) Unknown.\n\nBut according to the first general rule: "Kind things are cold." So since Gary is kind, he must be cold. Therefore, the statement "Gary is not cold" would be false. So the answer would be B) False. But wait, let me check if there\'s any conflicting information.\n\nWait, but let\'s check other rules. Let me see:.\n\nAnother rule is "All cold things are red." Since if Gary is cold (due to being kind), then he must be red. But Gary is already stated to be red, so that\'s okay. So that\'s consistent.\n\nAnother rule: "If something is white and nice then it is red." Doesn\'t affect Gary here.\n\n"Red, furry things are kind." Gary is red but we don\'t know if he\'s furry. Since Gary is kind, maybe that\'s covered by the first rule (kind implies cold), but the red and furry thing being kind is another way to be kind. But since Gary is not mentioned as furry, that\'s not necessary here.\n\nNow, the statement in question is "Gary is not cold." But according to the first rule, since Gary is kind, he must be cold. Therefore, the statement "Gary is not cold" is false. Therefore, the correct answer would be B) False.\n\nWait, but let me double-check for any possible contradictions or exceptions.\n\nIs there any rule that could allow Gary to be kind but not cold? The first rule says "Kind things are cold