a statement 18: Blue → sees lion. So mouse sees lion. Then, via statement 16: seeing the lion implies seeing the mouse (the mouse sees the mouse, which is okay). Then, via statement 15: if something sees the mouse, then mouse eats squirrel. Since the mouse sees the mouse (itself), that triggers that the mouse must eat the squirrel. So mouse.eats.squirrel is true.\n\nNow, since the mouse eats the squirrel, according to statement 11: If something (the mouse in this case) eats the squirrel, then the squirrel must visit the cow. Therefore, squirrel must visit the cow. So squirrel.visits.cow must be true.\n\nNow, the squirrel is already stated to visit the mouse (statement 10). So the squirrel visits both the mouse and the cow. Is that allowed?.\n\nStatement 14 says: If an animal visits the cow, then it cannot visit the squirrel. Wait, the squirrel is visiting the cow. So the squirrel (the same animal) is visiting the cow. Therefore, according to statement 14, the squirrel cannot visit the squirrel (itself?), but that\'s not the case here. Wait, the rule is: if X visits the cow, then X does not visit the squirrel. So if the squirrel visits the cow, then the squirrel cannot visit the squirrel (itself?), but that\'s a bit odd. Alternatively, maybe "visit the squirrel" here means visiting the squirrel animal (another entity). Wait, but the squirrel is visiting the cow, so according to statement 14, the squirrel cannot visit the squirrel (another animal?), but since the squirrel is an animal, that would mean it can\'t visit itself? That doesn\'t make sense. Alternatively, maybe it\'s a typo and should be "visits the cow, then it does not visit the mouse"? But no, the original statement is as given.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the rule is that if an animal visits the cow, it can\'t visit the squirrel (another animal). So if the squirrel visits the cow, then it can\'t visit the squirrel (the other animal?), but the squirrel is visiting the mouse, which is allowed. Wait, maybe th